return [
    // Sources: licence.source: Songs of the Four Nations, edited by Harold Boulton (Cramer, 1892), No. 26, pp. 134 to 138, printed in A-flat and written here in G; credits: words Harold Boulton (1892); tune traditional Welsh, "Ar Hyd y Nos" (printed 1784); licence: tune public domain, traditional, printed 1784; Boulton died in 1935; description: the first verse, sung softly like a hymn; the alto in thirds and sixths beneath; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'all-through-the-night' => 'All Through the Night is the Welsh lullaby "Ar Hyd y Nos", a traditional tune whose earliest known printing is from 1784, sung here with the English words Harold Boulton wrote for Songs of the Four Nations, which he edited in 1892. Our arrangement takes the melody and words from that book, moved from its A-flat down to G. It is the first verse for 2 to 4 singers, sung softly like a hymn. Every part sings the words together, note for note, so the chords move as one. The alto stays close under the tune, mostly in thirds and sixths, and the tenor and bass spend most of their time on a few notes.',
    // Sources: credits: words Robert Burns (1788); tune traditional Scottish; licence: tune public domain, traditional, published 1799 (no print named in licence.source); [The Conversation, "How Auld Lang Syne switched tunes en route to world domination"]; description: the first verse; bass holds the roots on "oo"; short, a good first try at holding your own line; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'auld-lang-syne' => 'Auld Lang Syne sets words Robert Burns sent to a friend in 1788, as an old song he admired, to a traditional Scottish tune. Burns\'s words have their earliest known printing in 1796, to a slow tune; the tune sung today joined them in 1799, in George Thomson\'s collection of Scottish airs. This arrangement is the first verse for 2 to 4 singers, and it is short. The alto and tenor sing the words with the tune, word for word, the tenor coming back to F again and again. The bass holds the roots on "oo" in a few long, low notes. A good first try at holding your own line.',
    // Sources: licence.source: words checked against Hull, The Poem-Book of the Gael (1912), p. 119, "A Prayer"; credits: words Irish, translated by Mary Byrne (1905), versified by Eleanor Hull (1912); tune "Slane", from P. W. Joyce, Old Irish Folk Music and Songs (1909); first set to these words in the Irish Church Hymnal (1919); licence: tune printed in Joyce (1909) as "With my love on the road"; Byrne died 1931, Hull died 1935; [Hymnology Archive, "Be Thou My Vision"]; description: alto and tenor move gently in the middle, the bass walks the roots; a stretch song; decisions.md row 2026-11-01 (Be Thou My Vision's C4 to F5, a stretch song); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'be-thou-my-vision' => 'Be Thou My Vision is an old Irish prayer poem, put into English by Mary Byrne in 1905 and into verse by Eleanor Hull in 1912. Our words follow Hull\'s printing in The Poem-Book of the Gael (1912). The tune, Slane, is an Irish folk melody that P. W. Joyce printed in 1909 as an air called With My Love on the Road; their earliest known pairing is in the Irish Church Hymnal of 1919. Here it is one verse for 2 to 4 singers, all on the words. The tune runs wide, so the alto and tenor move gently in the middle while the bass walks beneath.',
    // Sources: licence.source: melody from the score in Wikipedia, "Londonderry Air"; credits: words Frederic Weatherly (1913); tune Londonderry Air, Irish traditional (published by George Petrie, 1855); licence: Weatherly died 1929; [Minerva Classics, "A Source Guide to the Music of Percy Grainger" (Irish Tune from County Derry), quoting Petrie]; [The Methodist Church in Britain, "I cannot tell why he, whom angels worship (StF 350)", notes on the tune]; description: slow, a stretch song; decisions.md row 2026-11-01 (Danny Boy's range, a stretch song); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'danny-boy' => 'Danny Boy sets Frederic Weatherly\'s words of 1913 to the Londonderry Air, an Irish tune George Petrie published in 1855. Jane Ross of Limavady, who collected it, could say only that it was very old. This is one slow verse for 2 to 4 singers. The tune saves its highest notes for the second half and runs wider than most beginners find comfortable, so the other parts stay steady: the alto moves little, the tenor sits on A for most of the song, and the bass holds the low notes on "oo".',
    // Sources: licence.source: melody and words of the chorus from an abc transcription (trillian.mit.edu, "Let Me Call You Sweetheart [G]"), moved from G to B; credits: words Beth Slater Whitson (1910); tune Leo Friedman (1910); licence: Whitson died 1930, Friedman died 1927; description: the sweetheart waltz; alto and tenor lean into warm barbershop chords beneath the long held notes; bass sings the roots; decisions.md row 2026-11-02 (its chords go round the circle of fifths, G#7 C#7 F#7 B); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'let-me-call-you-sweetheart' => 'Let Me Call You Sweetheart is a waltz from 1910, with words by Beth Slater Whitson and music by Leo Friedman. It is for 2 to 4 singers, all singing the words together, note for note. The tune settles on long held notes at the end of its lines, and beneath them the alto and tenor lean into warm barbershop chords while the bass sings beneath.',
    // Sources: credits: words Latin hymn "Adeste Fideles" (18th century), English words Frederick Oakeley (1841) as revised in Murray's Hymnal (1852); tune attributed to John Francis Wade (c. 1743); licence: Oakeley died 1880 (no print named in licence.source); [Hymnology Archive, "Adeste Fideles"]; description: alto and tenor fill the chords, the bass climbs and falls beneath; bright, festive; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'o-come-all-ye-faithful' => 'O Come, All Ye Faithful is the Latin hymn Adeste Fideles, with a tune attributed to John Francis Wade, about 1743. Wade earned his living copying music by hand, and the hymn survives in several manuscripts he signed. The English words are Frederick Oakeley\'s from 1841, as revised in Murray\'s Hymnal of 1852. This arrangement is the first verse and the refrain for 2 to 4 singers, everyone on the words. The alto keeps mostly a third under the tune, the tenor fills the chords, and the bass climbs and falls beneath. Bright and festive.',
    // Sources: licence.source: melody from the score in Wikipedia, "Ode to Joy"; credits: words Henry van Dyke (1907); tune Ludwig van Beethoven, Symphony No. 9 (1824); licence: van Dyke died 1933; [Hymnology Archive, "Joyful, Joyful, We Adore Thee"]; description: alto moves beneath it, the bass holds the roots on "oo", the tenor fills the chord; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'ode-to-joy' => 'Ode to Joy is the tune from Beethoven\'s Ninth Symphony of 1824, sung here with Henry van Dyke\'s hymn words of 1907, Joyful, Joyful, We Adore Thee. In a letter, van Dyke said he wrote them with Beethoven\'s music always in his head. The arrangement is one verse for 2 to 4 singers. The tune moves almost entirely by step, so it is quick to pick up; the alto stays close beneath it, the tenor fills the chord, and the bass holds the low notes on "oo".',
    // Sources: licence.source: melody, words and chords from the score in Wikipedia, "Red River Valley"; credits: words traditional (Canadian and American, 1870s to 1890s); tune traditional; licence: words printed by 1896; [Manitoba Historical Society, Manitoba History no. 72 (2013), "The True Story of the Song 'Red River Valley'"]; description: the gentle cowboy song; easy to learn by ear; song file (the parts' guide notes); [Wikipedia, "Red River Valley" (The Weekly Wisconsin, Milwaukee, 1887)]
    'red-river-valley' => 'Red River Valley is a traditional song, Canadian and American, from the 1870s to the 1890s. The oldest known written copy of its words is a manuscript found in Iowa, marked with the year 1879, and the song was in print by 1887, in a Milwaukee newspaper. This is one verse for 2 to 4 singers. The gentle tune is easy to learn by ear; the alto and tenor sing the words with it, the alto often a sixth below, and the bass holds the low notes on "oo", on just three notes.',
    // Sources: licence.source: melody and words from E. Oram Lyte, The School Bell (Lancaster, Pa., 1893), No. 5, "Row, Row. Round.", read from archive.org/details/schoolbell150son00lyte (image n7), written a tone lower in C, its earliest printing seen the Hall and Lyte Teachers' Institute Glee Book (1874), "-ly" in "Gently" sung on D as commonly sung where the printings have E; credits: words traditional, the first two lines in print by 1852 (R. Sinclair, "Row, Row Your Boat, or the Old Log Hut", Firth, Pond & Co., 1852; University of Wisconsin-Madison, Mills Music Library, A02258), the round's words by 1881; tune credited to Eliphalet Oram Lyte (printed 1874); [the song facts' sources, checked by the courses lead: J. P. McCaskey, Franklin Square Song Collection (New York: Harper & Brothers, 1881), p. 69, "Row Your Boat. (Round.)", E. O. Lyte; Beth's Notes, "Row, Row, Row Your Boat" (music-teaching resource, 2013): "Harmony: one chord"]; description: everyone sings the same short tune three times, entering two bars apart (tenor and bass an octave lower); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'row-row-row' => 'Row, Row, Row Your Boat is a traditional nursery song whose first two lines were in print by 1852. The tune sung today was in print by 1874, as a round in a glee book for teachers by W. B. Hall and E. O. Lyte, and Lyte printed it again, signed with his initials, in his school songbook The School Bell (1893). Everyone sings the same short tune three times, entering two bars apart, with the tenor and bass pitched lower for lower voices. Leave out the quick notes and every note of the tune belongs to one chord, so any voice fits any other. A cheerful first round.',
    // Sources: credits: words traditional Ukrainian shchedrivka (New Year song); tune traditional Ukrainian folk melody; licence: tune the four-note motif; the folk tune and words as Leontovych printed them in Try pisni (Kharkiv, 1921), his part-writing not used (licence.source); his setting first sung in public in 1916 (the story: Makarenko, vn-bib, Ukrainer, ZN); [America Magazine, "The story of 'Carol of the Bells,' a Christmas classic born in Ukraine"]; [The song's own words (a shchedrivka, a New Year's well-wishing song); Interlude, "Carol of the Bells by Mykola Leontovych: History and Recordings"]; description: the alto joins in thirds and sixths, the bass and tenor ring slowly underneath like bells; song file (the parts' guide notes): sections Soprano alone, Alto joins, Bass joins, All parts; words in Ukrainian
    'shchedryk' => 'Shchedryk is a traditional Ukrainian shchedrivka, a New Year well-wishing song, in which a swallow flies into a household and tells the family how rich their coming year will be. It is built on one short four-note figure that keeps repeating while the other voices build around it. Mykola Leontovych\'s famous choral setting of it was first sung in public in 1916. We sing the folk tune and words as he printed them in 1921, in four parts of our own. Sung in Ukrainian by 2 to 4 singers, it builds part by part: the soprano starts alone, the alto joins, then the bass and the tenor ring slowly underneath.',
    // Sources: licence.source: melody from R. R. Terry, The Shanty Book, Part I (Curwen, 1921), No. 9 (archive.org/details/shantybooksailor00terr); "Sailors' Songs", The New Dominion Monthly (Montreal, April 1876), p. 262, prints "Shanadore" with its tune (canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.8_06581_104/24); credits: words traditional American (19th century); licence.words: which print has each line; the capstan: Terry files it under "Windlass and Capstan"; description: the long, rolling melody; slow and spacious, with time to breathe; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'shenandoah' => 'Shenandoah is a traditional American river song from the 19th century, in print by 1876, when it appeared as "Shanadore" in The New Dominion Monthly. At sea it became a capstan shanty, sung by sailors turning the big upright winch that hauled in the anchor. This is one verse for 2 to 4 singers, slow and spacious, with time to breathe. The soprano sings the long, rolling tune, the alto and tenor sing the words gently beneath it, and the bass holds the low notes on "oo".',
    // Sources: credits: words Joseph Mohr (1816), English words John Freeman Young (1859); tune Franz Xaver Gruber (1818); licence: German 1816, English 1859, tune 1818 (no print named in licence.source); [Hymnology Archive, "Stille Nacht / Silent Night"]; description: the alto a third below most of the way; a stretch song; decisions.md row 2026-11-01 (Silent Night's C4 to F5, a stretch song); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'silent-night' => 'Silent Night has German words by Joseph Mohr from 1816 and a tune by Franz Xaver Gruber, written when Mohr asked him for music for two solo voices and choir, with guitar. It was first sung that way at Christmas Eve Mass in 1818. The English words are John Freeman Young\'s from 1859. This arrangement is the first verse for 2 to 4 singers. The tune runs wide, from low C to high F, so take the edges gently or move the key. The alto sings a third below most of the way, the tenor holds G and C, and the bass holds the low notes on "oo".',
    // Sources: licence.source: Mary Hazzard's manuscript book "A Collection of Extra Songs of Various Kinds" (begun February 7th 1847), Winterthur Library, ASC 893, page image 57 (her page 37), headed "Simple Gifts.", undated and unsigned; credits: attributed to Joseph Brackett, usually dated 1848 (Andrews, 1940; the Concise Oxford Dictionary of Music, "probably comp. 1848"); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'simple-gifts' => 'Simple Gifts is a Shaker dancing song, usually credited to Elder Joseph Brackett and dated 1848, though the copy in Mary Hazzard\'s songbook is unsigned and undated. The words of its second half, about bowing, bending and turning, were dance directions. Our melody is read from that copy, now in the Winterthur Library, every pitch as she wrote it, moved from C to G. It is for 2 to 4 singers: the alto sings the words close beneath the tune and the tenor sings them lower, both on easy repeated notes, and the bass holds the low notes on "oo".',
    // Sources: licence.source: Theodore F. Seward, Jubilee Songs: As Sung by the Jubilee Singers of Fisk University (Biglow & Main, 1872), p. 28, in F; credits: traditional spiritual, attributed to Wallace Willis (published 1872); decisions.md row 2026-10-29 (keeps the book's spelling "hain't"; verse hummed by the alto, tenor and bass under the soprano's words); [W. E. B. Du Bois, The Souls of Black Folk (1903), "The Sorrow Songs" (Standard Ebooks edition)]; description: the refrain, the first verse and the refrain again; all joining on the last line; task brief: spirituals presented as songs of enslaved African Americans; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'steal-away' => 'Steal Away is a spiritual, one of the songs of enslaved African Americans, attributed to Wallace Willis. Our arrangement takes its melody and words from Theodore Seward\'s Jubilee Songs of 1872, the songs as sung by the Jubilee Singers of Fisk University, keeping the book\'s key of F and its spelling "hain\'t". W. E. B. Du Bois later called it the song of songs. For 2 to 4 singers, it has the refrain, the first verse and the refrain again. The refrain is in close harmony; in the verse the soprano sings the words while the others hum the chords, and everyone joins on the last line.',
    // Sources: licence.source: melody and words of the refrain from the 1903 M. Witmark and Sons sheet music, "You're the Flower of My Heart, Sweet Adeline" (Internet Archive, youreflowerofmyh00arms); credits: words Richard H. Gerard (1903); tune Harry Armstrong (1903); licence: Gerard died 1948, Armstrong died 1951; [National Park Service, "John Fitzgerald: Mayor of a Bigger, Better, Busier Boston"]; [American Heritage, "Honey Fitz"]; description: the barbershop favourite; alto and tenor slide through close chords, the bass walks the roots, a few ringing sevenths; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'sweet-adeline' => 'Sweet Adeline was published in 1903 as You\'re the Flower of My Heart, Sweet Adeline, with words by Richard H. Gerard and music by Harry Armstrong. Our melody and words of the refrain come from that 1903 sheet music, printed by M. Witmark and Sons. Boston\'s mayor John F. Fitzgerald, Honey Fitz, sang it at so many rallies that it became his theme song. This is a barbershop favourite for 2 to 4 singers, all on the words: the alto and tenor slide through close chords beneath the tune, and the bass walks beneath.',
    // Sources: licence.source: melody checked against "Swing Low, Sweet Chariot" ("Slave Hymn"), No. 48 in The One Hundred and One Best Songs (Chicago: The Cable Company, 1922 printing; arrangement copyright 1911 and 1919), archive.org/details/onehundredonebes0000unse_t3i3, also compared with Jubilee Songs (1872); credits: traditional spiritual, attributed to Wallace Willis (published 1872); licence: words published 1872 (Jubilee Songs, Fisk Jubilee Singers); [Theodore F. Seward and George L. White, Jubilee Songs (New York: Biglow & Main, 1872), preface p. 3 and p. 6, Emory University facsimile, archive.org/details/29208201.4853.emory.edu]; [Judith Michener, "Willis, 'Uncle' Wallace and 'Aunt' Minerva", The Encyclopedia of Oklahoma History and Culture, entry WI018]; [Oklahoma State Senate, "Senate sings praises of Swing Low, Sweet Chariot as state gospel song", 24 February 2011]; [Hymnary.org, Psalter Hymnal (1987), No. 617, with the Psalter Hymnal Handbook notes]; [Carnegie Hall, Timeline of African American Music, "Concert Spiritual"]; description: the chorus, the first verse and the chorus again; task brief: spirituals presented as songs of enslaved African Americans; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'swing-low' => 'Swing Low, Sweet Chariot is a spiritual, one of the songs of enslaved African Americans, traditionally attributed to Wallace Willis and printed in 1872 in Jubilee Songs, as sung by the Fisk Jubilee Singers. The tune uses just five notes, and it is built for call and response. For 2 to 4 singers, it has the chorus, the first verse and the chorus again. The alto and tenor sing the words with the tune, and the bass holds the low notes on "oo".',
    // Sources: licence.source: melody and words from Sharp and Marson, Folk Songs from Somerset, third series (Simpkin, 1906), no. 66, "O Waly, Waly"; credits: words as collected by Cecil Sharp and Charles Marson (1906), verses 1 and 6; tune sung by Mrs Cox of High Ham, collected by Cecil Sharp (1906); licence: Sharp died 1924, Marson 1914; [Just Another Tune, "The Water Is Wide" (quoting Sharp's note in Folk Songs From Somerset, Third Series, 1906)]; description: two verses, slow and lyrical; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'the-water-is-wide' => 'The Water Is Wide is a traditional folk song; an older relative, Waly, Waly, was in print by the 1720s. Cecil Sharp heard it five times in Somerset, with tunes and words varying a lot. Our melody and words come from Sharp and Charles Marson\'s Folk Songs from Somerset, third series (1906), song 66, O Waly, Waly, its tune as sung by Mrs Cox of High Ham. This arrangement is two verses for 2 to 4 singers, slow and lyrical: the alto and tenor sing the words beneath the smooth tune, and the bass holds the low notes on "oo".',
    // Sources: licence.source: melody and words read from New Jubilee Songs, 2nd ed. (1904), pages 8 and 9, transposed from E minor to F minor; the 1904 scan (title page; pp. 8 and 9: one staff, no harmony; heading "A favorite melody among Southern Baptists while a baptism is in progress"); Catalogue of Title Entries, 3 July 1902, p. 677, entry 10110 (registered 19 June 1902); Canterbury Dictionary of Hymnology, "Frederick Jerome Work" and "John Wesley Work (II)" (John's name does not appear in the book); description: the chorus, two verses and the chorus between and after; the soprano calls and the others answer; the bass on "doom, ba-doom"; a stretch song; task brief: spirituals presented as songs of enslaved African Americans; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'wade-in-the-water' => 'Wade in the Water is a spiritual, one of the songs of enslaved African Americans. Its earliest printing we have read is in New Jubilee Songs as Sung by the Fisk Jubilee Singers, collected and harmonized by Frederick J. Work: the book\'s second edition of 1904 (the first came out in June 1902) prints it as a bare tune, a favorite melody at baptisms. Our melody and words are read from that page, moved from E minor up to F minor. For 2 to 4 singers, it has the chorus, two verses and the chorus between and after. In the verses the soprano calls and the others answer, while the bass keeps a steady "doom, ba-doom" underneath.',
    // Sources: credits: words John Newton (1779); tune "New Britain", American folk hymn (1829); licence: words published 1779, tune published 1829 (no print named in licence.source); [Hymnology Archive, "Amazing grace!", and Hymnary.org: Olney Hymns (1779); the tune's earliest known printing, Columbian Harmony (1829); a letter of 1828 is a single source and left out]; [The tune's own notes (G, A, B, D and E)]; description: a gentle first harmony; the bass holds the roots on "oo"; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'amazing-grace' => 'Amazing Grace sets John Newton\'s words, printed in 1779 in Olney Hymns, to New Britain, an American folk hymn tune. The tune\'s earliest known printing is in Columbian Harmony, in 1829, a book whose notes have shapes that show their place in the scale. It uses only five different notes. This arrangement is the first verse for 2 to 4 singers, a gentle first harmony: the alto moves a third or a sixth beneath the tune on almost every note, the tenor holds almost every note on G, and the bass holds the low notes on "oo".',
    // Sources: tune: [C*** (Pierre Adolphe Capelle), La Clé du Caveau (Paris: Capelle et Renand, 1811), air No. 726, p. 309, and table p. 57, archive.org/details/laclducaveau00cape, no words; The Ballad Index, "Frere Jacques" (FSWB412F), from James J. Fuld, The Book of World-Famous Music: earliest known printing 1811]; English words: Peter W. Dykema, "Practical Suggestions for The National Week of Song", Normal Instructor and Primary Plans 25, no. 4 (February 1916), pp. 21 to 22, archive.org/details/sim_instructor_1916-02_25_4 (the earliest printing we have found); matins, the night prayers: [Dictionnaire de l'Académie française, 6th edition (1835), "Matines"; Fernand Cabrol, "Matins", The Catholic Encyclopedia, vol. 10 (1911)]; description: everyone sings the same tune, entering two bars apart (tenor and bass an octave lower); the easiest way into singing parts; song file (the parts' guide notes); story are-you-sleeping, checked 2026-11-13
    'are-you-sleeping' => 'Are You Sleeping is the English version of Frère Jacques, a traditional French tune whose earliest known printing, without words, is from Paris in 1811; the English words were in print by 1916. In the original words, the sleeping brother is late to ring the bells for matins, the night prayers, and the ding, dang, dong is those bells. Here it is a round for 2 to 4 singers: everyone sings the same tune twice, entering two bars apart, with the tenor and bass pitched lower for lower voices, and the harmony happens by itself. The easiest way into singing parts.',
    // Sources: licence.source: melody and words from Slave Songs of the United States, ed. William Francis Allen, Charles Pickard Ware and Lucy McKim Garrison (New York: A. Simpson & Co., 1867), p. 23, No. 31, "Michael row the boat ashore", in E-flat in 2/4, written here a tone higher in F, scan at archive.org/details/slavesongsofunit00alle (leaf n82); credits: traditional spiritual (Slave Songs of the United States, 1867); [the song facts' sources, checked by the courses lead: Wesleyan University, History and Traditions (trivia); Official Charts Company, "Michael" (1961); number1sblog (UK number ones); Associated Press obituary of Dave Fisher (2010); William Francis Allen, introduction to Slave Songs of the United States (New York: A. Simpson, 1867), p. xvi; Jeff Place, notes to Pete Seeger: The Smithsonian Folkways Collection (SFW 40225, 2019), p. 62]; description: a short, easy tune with a "Hallelujah" to answer; the alto stays close by, mostly on one held note; task brief: spirituals presented as songs of enslaved African Americans; song file (the parts' guide notes); story michael-row, checked 2026-11-13
    'michael-row' => 'Michael, Row the Boat Ashore is a spiritual, one of the songs of enslaved African Americans, printed in 1867 in Slave Songs of the United States. The Michael of the song is usually taken to be the archangel Michael. It is a short, easy tune with a Hallelujah to answer each line, for 2 to 4 singers: the alto stays close by, mostly on one held note, the tenor fills the chord, and the bass holds the low notes on "oo".',
    // Sources: credits: words Traditional (published 1911); tune "Good Morning to All", Mildred J. Hill (1893); licence: words public domain, the US copyright claim was ruled invalid (2015), expired in the EU (2017); tune published 1893; [the song facts' sources, checked by the courses lead: Marya v. Warner/Chappell Music, Inc., No. CV 13-4460-GHK, US District Court, Central District of California, order of 22 September 2015, pp. 1-2; and IMSLP, Happy Birthday to You (Hill, Mildred); Marya v. Warner/Chappell Music, Inc., No. CV 13-4460-GHK (C.D. Cal., 22 September 2015), conclusion, p. 43; and Berkeley Technology Law Journal, "The original happy song: now free for all" (December 2015); Musicalities Ltd (UK music copyright consultancy), "Do I have to pay to record Happy Birthday?"; and Stephen Carlisle, "Happy Birthday is in the public domain?", Nova Southeastern University (2015); Renee C. Quinn and Brian Focarino, IPWatchdog (28 October 2015); and Joyce Klemmer, Smith Gambrell & Russell, "The Happy Birthday song: how old are you now?" (Mondaq / Legal 500, 16 September 2015)]; decisions.md row 2026-10-27 ("dear friend" for the name); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'happy-birthday' => 'Happy Birthday takes its tune from Good Morning to All, a morning greeting for a kindergarten class, whose earliest known printing is in 1893, in Song Stories for the Kindergarten by the sisters Mildred and Patty Hill. The birthday words are traditional and were published in 1911. This arrangement is for 2 to 4 singers, with "dear friend" in place of a name: the alto sings mostly a third or sixth below, the tenor fills the chord, and the bass holds the low notes on "oo".',
    // Sources: licence.source: Daisy Bell, song, written and composed by Harry Dacre (T. B. Harms & Co., New York, and Francis, Day & Hunter, London, copyright 1892), pp. 3 to 5, in G, archive.org/details/daisybell00harr; credits: words and tune Harry Dacre (1892; died 1922); song facts: Tempo di Valse, the chorus marked a little faster (the same sheet music); decisions.md row 2026-11-01 (verse 1 and the chorus, our close harmony on Dacre's chords)
    'daisy-bell' => 'Daisy Bell, the song many know as A Bicycle Built for Two, was written and composed by Harry Dacre and published in 1892. Our arrangement is read from a copy of that sheet music, published by T. B. Harms in New York and Francis, Day and Hunter in London and printed in 1893 or later, in its own key of G. It is a waltz, marked Tempo di Valse, and its chorus is marked to go a little faster than the verse. This is the first verse and the chorus for 2 to 4 singers, all on the words: the tune on top, the alto close beneath it, mostly on the third of each chord, the tenor filling the chord and the bass beneath.',
    // Sources: [Isaac Watts, The Psalms of David Imitated in the Language of the New Testament (London: J. Clark, R. Ford and R. Cruttenden, 1719), pp. 250 and 252 to 254, archive.org/details/chrtwimi00watt: Psalm 98; Hymnary.org, "Isaac Watts" (from Julian's Dictionary of Hymnology, 1907)]; song facts [Mason, Occasional Psalm and Hymn Tunes, No. 3 (Boston, copyright 1836), No. 73, "Antioch", p. 70: his earliest known printing of the tune, "Arranged from Handel", the tenor and bass already behind on "And heav'n and nature sing"]; [Hymnology Archive, "Joy to the World": the tune printed in English tune books of the early 1830s as COMFORT; the Cyber Hymnal: English tune books of about 1833 to 1835]; song facts [Mason, The Modern Psalmist (1839), p. 144, in D]; licence.source: Mason and Webb, The National Psalmist (Boston, 1848), p. 132, archive.org/details/nationalpsalmist00maso; song facts (1848 tenor and bass a bar behind); decisions.md row 2026-11-07 (in D, verse 1)
    'joy-to-the-world' => 'Joy to the World sets Isaac Watts\'s words, published in 1719 in his Psalms of David and based on Psalm 98, to the tune Antioch, which Lowell Mason published in 1836, arranging an English tune printed a few years before. Our arrangement is read from Mason\'s own four parts in The National Psalmist of 1848, in D, the key of his Modern Psalmist of 1839. This is the first verse for 2 to 4 singers: the tune runs straight down the scale on "Joy to the world", and on "And heav\'n and nature sing" the tenor and bass come in a bar behind the others, as Mason already printed it in 1836 and as choirs still sing it today.',
    // Sources: [John and Charles Wesley, Hymns and Sacred Poems (London: Strahan, 1739), p. 206, "Hymn for Christmass-Day", facsimile at archive.org; George Whitefield, A Collection of Hymns for Social Worship (London, 1753), Hymn XXXI, the opening couplet, facsimile at archive.org; Chris Fenner, "Hark how all the welkin rings", Hymnology Archive (2018, revised 2022), and Hymnary.org, MENDELSSOHN (tune): Mendelssohn's Festgesang of 1840 and W. H. Cummings's fitting of it]; licence.source: Hymns Ancient and Modern (ed. W. H. Monk, 1875), hymn 60; decisions.md row 2026-11-07 (in G at 92, verse 1 and the refrain)
    'hark-the-herald' => 'Hark! The Herald Angels Sing began as Charles Wesley\'s Hymn for Christmas-Day of 1739, and George Whitefield rewrote its opening lines in 1753. The tune is Felix Mendelssohn\'s, from a festival song of 1840, fitted to Wesley\'s words by William H. Cummings in the 1850s. Our arrangement is read from the four parts of Hymns Ancient and Modern, in its standard edition of 1875, in G as printed. This is the first verse and the refrain for 2 to 4 singers, all on the words, bright and festive, with a breath at every line.',
    // Sources: song facts [J. Pierpont, The One Horse Open Sleigh: Song and Chorus (Boston: Oliver Ditson & Co., 1857), cover and pp. 3 to 5, "Deposited Sept. 16. 1857", Library of Congress copy, facsimile: a sleigh-ride song with no word of Christmas]; [the courses lead's story check: today's chorus already in Carmina Collegensia (Boston: Ditson, 1876), "accompanied by jingling glasses", and in an 1887 college songbook]; licence.source: Heart Songs Dear to the American People (Chapple, Boston, 1909), pp. 148 to 149, and Songs the Whole World Sings (Appleton, 1915), p. 177; decisions.md row 2026-11-07 (in G at 120, verse 1 and the chorus, "dm" and "jing")
    'jingle-bells' => 'Jingle Bells was written by James Lord Pierpont and published in 1857 as The One Horse Open Sleigh: a song about a sleigh ride, with no word of Christmas, which became a Christmas song later. Our arrangement follows the verse and chorus as printed in Heart Songs of 1909, with today\'s chorus, which college songbooks printed from 1876, set by Stackapella on that page\'s harmony, in G. This is the first verse and the chorus for 2 to 4 singers, bright and quick: the tune on top, the alto mostly a sixth under it, the tenor filling the chord, and the bass trotting on "dm" in the verse and ringing "jing" in the chorus.',
    // Sources: song facts [Bramley and Stainer, Christmas Carols New and Old, first series (Novello, 1867; an 1870s printing), No. 1 and its index: words and tune traditional]; [The Musical Times, 1 December 1867, no. 298: the first series among Novello's books of the last month]; licence.source: the same, archive.org/details/christmascarolsn00staiiala; decisions.md row 2026-11-07 (in E minor at 120, verse 1 and the refrain, "God rest you" as printed)
    'god-rest-ye' => 'God Rest Ye Merry, Gentlemen is a traditional English carol: nobody knows who first wrote its words or its tune. It is carol number 1 in Christmas Carols New and Old, edited by H. R. Bramley and John Stainer, whose first series came out in 1867; our arrangement is read from Stainer\'s four parts in a printing of the 1870s, in E minor as printed, with the words as the book gives them, "God rest you, merry gentlemen". This is the first verse and its refrain for 2 to 4 singers: on "O tidings of comfort and joy" the harmony brightens into G major before the last "joy" comes home to E minor.',
    // Sources: song facts [John Parry, British Harmony (1781), p. 12, No. 7, "Nôs Calan", and Edward Jones, Musical and Poetical Relicks of the Welsh Bards (1784), p. 64, "Nôs Galan", read from the scans]; song facts [Thomas Oliphant's English words, 1862]; licence.source: The Golden Book of Favorite Songs (Hall and McCreary, 1923), p. 66, "Deck The Hall"; decisions.md row 2026-11-07 (in E-flat at 120, verse 1)
    'deck-the-halls' => 'Deck the Halls is sung to a Welsh tune, Nos Galan, which means New Year\'s Eve; it was in print by 1781, in John Parry\'s British Harmony, and the harpist Edward Jones printed it in 1784. The English words are by the Scottish musician Thomas Oliphant, printed in 1862 in a book of Welsh melodies; the version sung today, with "Don we now our gay apparel", is a revision whose earliest known printing is in an American school journal of 1877. Our arrangement is read from the four parts of The Golden Book of Favorite Songs, an American songbook of 1923, in E-flat. This is the first verse for 2 to 4 singers, bright and quick: the alto sings most of the "fa la la"s a third under the tune, and the rising "fa la la" in the middle climbs to the song\'s top note.',
    // Sources: [The Christian Cynosure (Chicago), 2 March 1882, p. 11, "Luther's Cradle Song", facsimile at archive.org: in print by 1882; James R. Murray, Dainty Songs for Little Lads and Lasses (1887), p. 110, "Luther's Cradle Hymn"; Around the World with Christmas (1895), p. 11, Kirkpatrick's tune, his earliest known printing; both reproduced in Chris Fenner, "Away in a manger", Hymnology Archive (2018, revised 2023)]; [The Canterbury Dictionary of Hymnology, "Away in a manger": Kirkpatrick's tune the usual one in Britain; Richard S. Hill (1945): James R. Murray's setting the one most printed in the United States]; licence.source: The Church Hymnal Revised, ed. Charles L. Hutchins (1920), No. 643, archive.org/details/churchhymnalrevi00hutc, checked against Kirkpatrick and Fillmore, Joy and Praise ([1908]), No. 174; the tenor and bass ours; decisions.md row 2026-11-07 (in G at 84, verse 1, the tenor ours)
    'away-in-a-manger' => 'Away in a Manger is an anonymous American carol, in print by 1882. Early books credited its words to Martin Luther, but historians reject that. The tune is William J. Kirkpatrick\'s, whose earliest known printing is of 1895 and which was later called Cradle Song; the Canterbury Dictionary of Hymnology calls it the usual tune in Britain, while in the United States the setting most printed is James R. Murray\'s, often called Mueller. Our arrangement is read from The Church Hymnal Revised of 1920, edited by Charles L. Hutchins, which prints Kirkpatrick\'s two voices: the soprano and alto sing them, and the tenor and bass are ours, on his harmony. This is the first verse for 2 to 4 singers, in G.',
    // Sources: credits: words Numbers 6:24 to 26 (King James Version), music Peter C. Lutkin (1900); licence.source: Lutkin's octavo (Clayton F. Summy, Chicago, 1900, plate 524-3), read from a later Summy printing of the 1900 plates, checked against The Methodist Hymnal (1905), No. 748; song facts [the song facts' sources, checked by the courses lead: Northwestern University Bienen School of Music, "History"; Chris Fenner, "The Lord bless you and keep you", Hymnology Archive (2018)]; decisions.md row 2026-11-08 (in C, 4 only, no pitch changed); song file (the Amen's entries)
    'the-lord-bless-you' => 'The Lord Bless You and Keep You is Peter C. Lutkin\'s choral blessing of 1900, set to the words of Numbers 6:24 to 26 in the King James Version. Lutkin was the first dean of Northwestern University\'s School of Music. Our arrangement is read from Summy\'s octavo of 1900, in a later printing from the same plates, in C, with every pitch as he wrote it, and checked against The Methodist Hymnal of 1905, where it is No. 748. It is for 4 singers: the blessing sung together, slow and smooth, then his sevenfold Amen, where the tenor comes in first, then the bass, the alto and the soprano, rising to the soprano\'s high G.',
    // Sources: credits: words Brewster Higley (1872 or 1873), tune Daniel E. Kelley (1870s); [Wikipedia, "Home on the Range": Higley of Smith County, Kansas, wrote "My Western Home"; Kelley, a friend, set the melody; Kansas state song, June 30, 1947]; song facts [Kansas Historical Society, Kansapedia, "Home on the Range": Lomax recorded a saloon keeper in 1908 who had driven cattle up the Chisholm Trail]; licence.source: Lomax, Cowboy Songs and Other Frontier Ballads (Sturgis & Walton, 1910), pp. 39 to 40, printed in E-flat in 6/8, written here in G in 3/4; description: the verse and the refrain, as a gentle waltz; the bass on "oo", with the words only for "Home, home on the range"; decisions.md row 2026-10-29 (the source, Lomax's first edition); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'home-on-the-range' => 'Home on the Range began as My Western Home, a poem that Brewster Higley, a doctor in Smith County, Kansas, wrote in the early 1870s, and his friend Daniel E. Kelley is credited with setting it to music. In 1908 the folk song collector John Lomax recorded a saloon keeper singing it, a man who had once been a cook on cattle drives up the Chisholm Trail. By the 1930s the song\'s origins were obscure and widely debated, until a lawsuit over it in 1934 set off a search that traced it back to Higley and Kelley, and in 1947 it became the state song of Kansas. Our arrangement is read from Lomax\'s Cowboy Songs and Other Frontier Ballads of 1910, which prints it in E-flat in 6/8; we sing it in G, as a gentle waltz. This is the verse and the refrain for 2 to 4 singers: the tune on top, the alto a third or a sixth under it on the words, the tenor filling the chord, mostly on G, and the bass holding long low notes on "oo", singing the words only for "Home, home on the range".',
    // Sources: story [Brown's Book-Stall, No. 75 (Aberdeen, December 1911), pp. 5 and 7, quoting Gavin Greig: the earliest known printing a song sheet, Bonnie Loch Loman, Paterson & Roy, Edinburgh, about 1840, the song having circulated in manuscript before it; the Ballad Index: earliest date 1841]; song facts [Alfred Moffat, The Minstrelsy of Scotland, p. 34, note: the air from Kind Robin lo'es me, printed 1742]; song facts [Classical-Music.com, "Loch Lomond lyrics": Vaughan Williams's 1921 setting for unaccompanied choir]; song facts [The Syncopated Times, "Maxine Sullivan": the 1937 swing version made her famous]; credits: words and tune traditional Scottish; story [The Illustrated London News, 11 July 1885, p. 49, and Chambers's Journal, 25 July 1885, p. 476: Songs of the North new from Field and Tuer]; licence.source: John Reid, Songs of Scotland (Boosey, 1908), pp. 228 to 229, printed in E and written here in G, checked against John Greig, Scots Minstrelsie, vol. III (undated, 1892 to 1895), pp. 352 to 353; sections: Verse 1, Chorus, Verse 2, Chorus; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'loch-lomond' => 'Loch Lomond is a traditional Scottish song. Its earliest known printing is an Edinburgh song sheet of about 1840, but the song is probably older than that, and its tune may be older than its words. Ralph Vaughan Williams set it for unaccompanied choir in 1921, and in 1937 Maxine Sullivan\'s swing version made her famous. Our arrangement is read from John Reid\'s Songs of Scotland of 1908, checked against John Greig\'s Scots Minstrelsie of 1893, and moved from Reid\'s E up to G. This is two verses, each with the chorus "O ye\'ll tak\' the high road", for 2 to 4 singers, everyone on the words: the alto a third or a sixth under the tune, the tenor filling the chord, mostly on G, and the bass on a low G for more than half the song, like a drone, moving to C and D where the chords change.',
    // Sources: credits: tune Johannes Brahms, Op. 49 No. 4 (1868); words Arthur Westbrook (1901), after Natalia Macfarren's English (about 1874), from the German; licence: "Arthur Westbrook" listed by the Library of Congress (after Drone, Musical AKAs) as a pen name of Charles Fonteyn Manney; IMSLP names Arthur Edward Westbrook (died 1952); song facts [the song facts' sources, checked by the courses lead: OMI facsimile of the autograph presentation copy (sent 15 July 1868 to Arthur and Bertha Faber for the birth of their second son, Hans); and Georg Predota, "Sing, sleep, honey", Interlude (2016); Brahms-Institut an der Musikhochschule Lübeck, Werkekatalog, Op. 49 (first documented performance); and Max Kalbeck, Johannes Brahms, vol. 2 (1908), p. 331]; licence.source: the first edition (N. Simrock, Berlin, 1868), p. 11, in E-flat; the English words from Forty Songs, ed. James Huneker (Ditson, 1903), which also checked the notes; the first edition one verse with a repeat sign; verse 2 and both English verses from the Ditson book; licence.arrangement: the tenor and bass on "oo" from his left hand's chords; description: slow and gentle, the alto under the tune on the same words; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'brahms-lullaby' => 'Brahms\'s Lullaby is the Wiegenlied, Op. 49 No. 4, that Johannes Brahms published in 1868, a gift for his friend Bertha Faber on the birth of her second son. Its first known public performance was in Vienna, sung by Luise Dustmann with Clara Schumann at the piano. Our arrangement is read from the first edition (Simrock, Berlin, 1868), in Brahms\'s key of E-flat, with Arthur Westbrook\'s English, which keeps Natalia Macfarren\'s opening "Lullaby and good night", as a Ditson book printed it in 1903. This is both verses for 2 to 4 singers, slow and gentle: the tune on top, the alto in thirds and sixths below, and the tenor and bass on soft "oo" chords from Brahms\'s piano part.',
    // Sources: song facts [[Thomas Ravenscroft, compiler], Pammelia (London: William Barley, 1609), p. 41, No. 85, read from the facsimile (the 1971 Da Capo Press reprint, also on IMSLP), and William H. Husk, "Pammelia", in Grove, A Dictionary of Music and Musicians, vol. 2 (1880), p. 643: the earliest printed English book of rounds and catches, credited to Thomas Ravenscroft, a round for five voices; the 1609 words end "fill the pot, Eadie", "yet will I be merry" much later]; credits: words and tune anonymous (printed 1609); licence.source: the facsimile at pbm.com/~lindahl/ravenscroft/pammelia/pam_44.gif, a canon at the unison for five voices, written here in F minor (a minor third higher) for up to four voices entering a bar apart; description: tenor and bass an octave lower, the harmony happens by itself, the words in today's spelling; decisions.md row 2026-11-01 (each singing it three times); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'hey-ho-nobody-home' => 'Hey, Ho, Nobody Home is a round printed in 1609 in Pammelia, the earliest printed English book of rounds and catches, credited to Thomas Ravenscroft; nobody knows who wrote its words or its tune. There it is a round for five voices, and its words end "fill the pot, Eadie": a call to fill the drinking pot. The ending many people know today, "yet will I be merry", came later. Our round is read from a facsimile of the 1609 page, with its words in today\'s spelling, written in F minor, a minor third above the print. Here it is for 2 to 4 singers: everyone sings the same short tune three times, entering a bar apart, with the tenor and bass pitched an octave lower for lower voices, and the harmony happens by itself.',
    // Sources: credits: words Horatio G. Spafford (printed 1876); tune Philip P. Bliss, "Ville du Havre" (1876); [Library of Congress, The American Colony in Jerusalem (exhibition, 2005), "Family Tragedy": his four daughters, Annie, Maggie, Bessie and Tanetta; Chris Fenner, "It is well with my soul", Hymnology Archive (2018, revised 2026)]; song facts [Gospel Hymns No. 2 (1876), No. 76, read from the scan: the songbook Bliss and Sankey made for their revival meetings; our four parts read from that page, the alto and tenor lightly revoiced; the echo in the chorus written into the parts]; licence.source: the same page, in Db and written here in C; licence.arrangement: the alto and tenor revoiced so 2 and 3 singers keep each chord's third; description: the first verse and the chorus, all four sing the last line together, steady and warm; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'it-is-well' => 'It Is Well with My Soul has words by Horatio G. Spafford, whose four daughters were killed when the ship Ville du Havre sank in November 1873. His family said he wrote them as his ship passed the place where they died; other accounts put the writing in Chicago, before the voyage or in 1876. Philip P. Bliss\'s tune is named Ville du Havre, after the ship. Its earliest known printing is No. 76 in Gospel Hymns No. 2 (1876), and our arrangement is read from Bliss\'s four parts on that page, in C, lightly revoiced. This is the first verse and the chorus for 2 to 4 singers, steady and warm: in the chorus the tune holds "well" and "soul" while the others answer beneath it.',
    // Sources: credits: words Reginald Heber (1826); tune John Bacchus Dykes, "Nicaea" (1861); [Julian, A Dictionary of Hymnology (1892), "Heber, Reginald", p. 503, and Heber, Hymns, Written and Adapted to the Weekly Church Service of the Year (John Murray, 1827), preface and p. 84: written at Hodnet, published by his widow in 1827; Heber, Bampton Lectures (1816), title page, and Amelia Heber, Life of Reginald Heber (1830), vol. 1, p. 423: rector of Hodnet; Julian (1907), New Supplement, p. 1649: the earliest known printing, the Banbury Selection, 3rd ed., 1826]; song facts [Hymns Ancient and Modern (Novello, 1861), Hymn 135, read from the scan: the hymn for Trinity Sunday; the index marks Nicaea as a new tune; Dykes's own four parts]; licence.source: the same, in E, printed in minims and written here in crotchets, the Amen left out; licence.arrangement: the alto and tenor revoiced so the duet and trio are complete; description: the first verse, steady and broad, every line one breath; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'holy-holy-holy' => 'Holy, Holy, Holy is Reginald Heber\'s hymn for Trinity Sunday, written while he was rector of Hodnet in Shropshire. Its earliest known printing is from 1826, the year he died in India, and his widow published it in his Hymns of 1827. Its tune, Nicaea, is John Bacchus Dykes\'s, written for Hymns Ancient and Modern in 1861, where the hymn is No. 135. Our arrangement is read from that page, in E as printed, on Dykes\'s harmony, with the alto and tenor revoiced for small groups and no Amen. This is the first verse for 2 to 4 singers, steady and broad: the tune climbs the home chord on "Holy, holy, holy", and the bass sings Dykes\'s line, made easier in three spots: it stays on the upper A on "Lord" and "merci-", where Dykes sings the low A and leaps the octave, and sings B, the chord\'s root, on "Early", where Dykes has D-sharp.',
    // Sources: [KJV, Exodus 5:1: Moses and Aaron tell Pharaoh, "Thus saith the Lord God of Israel, Let my people go"]; [The Song of the "Contrabands": "O Let My People Go" (Horace Waters, 1861), Lockwood's notes p. 2 (originated among the "Contrabands", first heard at Fortress Monroe) and p. 5 (sung for about nine years in Virginia), verse 10 "O let us all from bondage flee", Filson Historical Society, 781.62 B168s]; [Zoellner, The Road Was Full of Thorns (The New Press, 2025), quoting Lockwood: the refugees had "a deep impression they were the second children of Israel"]; [Douglass, My Bondage and My Freedom (1855), ch. XIX: "the north was our Canaan"]; song facts [Seward, Jubilee Songs (Biglow & Main, 1872), pp. 22 to 23, read from the scan: twenty-four verses, from Israel in Egypt to the Red Sea and on to freedom; only the answer, "Let my people go", in four-part harmony]; credits: traditional spiritual, as sung by the Fisk Jubilee Singers (published 1872); licence.source: the same book, p. 22, in F minor and written here a tone up in G minor; verses 1 and 2, each with the refrain; decisions.md row 2026-11-02 (words as printed, "Tell ole Pha-roh"; the soprano sings every call alone); description: call and answer, slow and steady, in a minor key; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'go-down-moses' => 'Go Down, Moses is an African-American spiritual that tells the story of the Exodus, in which God sends Moses to tell Pharaoh, "Let my people go". Enslaved African Americans heard in it their own bondage, like the Israelites\' in Egypt. The Fisk Jubilee Singers\' book of 1872, Theodore F. Seward\'s Jubilee Songs, gives it twenty-four verses, from Israel in Egypt to the Red Sea and on to freedom, and writes only the answer, "Let my people go", in four-part harmony; the rest is the tune alone, each verse\'s call for one voice and "Go down, Moses" doubled in octaves. Our arrangement is read from that page, a tone higher, in G minor, with its words as printed, "Tell ole Pharoh". For 2 to 4 singers, it is two verses, each with the refrain, slow and steady, in call and answer: the soprano sings every call alone, and everyone answers "Let my people go" in harmony, the alto and tenor on two notes each and the bass on D and a low G.',
    // Sources: credits: words Ada Blenkhorn (1899), tune J. Howard Entwisle (1899); song facts [Hymnary.org, "There's a dark and a troubled side of life": Bright Melodies (1899), a Philadelphia songbook by John R. Sweney and Entwisle, who wrote its music]; song facts [Smithsonian Folkways, liner notes by Hal Bruno (FTS 32431): the Carter Family recorded it in Camden, New Jersey, in 1928, and it became their familiar theme song]; licence.source: Heavenly Sunlight (MacCalla & Co., Philadelphia, 1900), No. 40, "Keep on the Sunny Side of Life", in A-flat; decisions.md rows 2026-10-29 (Entwisle's parts kept except where the alto takes the third and the tenor the alto's note) and 2026-11-01 (only clearly hard spots in Entwisle's parts changed); description: the first verse and the chorus, bright and bouncing, the other parts on the words with the tune; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'keep-on-the-sunny-side' => 'Keep on the Sunny Side has words by Ada Blenkhorn and music by J. Howard Entwisle, and it appeared in 1899 in Bright Melodies, a Philadelphia songbook by John R. Sweney and Entwisle. The Carter Family recorded it in Camden, New Jersey, in 1928, and it became their familiar theme song. Our arrangement is read from Entwisle\'s own four parts in Heavenly Sunlight, a songbook of 1900, where it is called Keep on the Sunny Side of Life, in A-flat as printed, kept close to how he wrote them, with the alto taking the chord\'s third from the tenor in a few places. This is the first verse and the chorus for 2 to 4 singers, bright and bouncing, everyone on the words: the alto a sixth or a third under the tune, the tenor filling the chord, mostly on A-flat and E-flat, and the bass on the roots, mostly A-flat and E-flat.',
    // Sources: song facts [The Traditional Ballad Index, "Down by the Old Mill Stream", Notes: third most popular song in America in 1911; Spaeth, A History of Popular Music in America (1948), p. 372, the comic gestures]; song facts [IMSLP, "Down by the Old Mill Stream (Taylor, Tell)": the 1910 sheet music's men's-voices setting of the chorus]; credits: words and tune Tell Taylor (1910); licence.source: the first edition (Tell Taylor, Chicago, 1910), chorus "Valse lento", printed in G, written here in A at men's pitch, archive.org/details/sm_downbytheoldmillstream; decisions.md rows 2026-11-01 (true barbershop; Hillel: "Only Old Mill Stream" changes key); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'down-by-the-old-mill-stream' => 'Down by the Old Mill Stream is a waltz song by Tell Taylor, published in Chicago in 1910, and a later chart history ranks it the third most popular song in America in 1911. Its early sheet music already came with a setting of the chorus for men\'s voices, and by 1948 the music historian Sigmund Spaeth noted that audiences were being taught to sing it with comic gestures. Our arrangement is read from an early printing of it, voiced for barbershop and moved from G up to A. This is the chorus, for 2 to 4 singers: the lead sings the tune, the tenor rings above it, the baritone fills the chord and the bass holds the roots.',
    // Sources: song facts [Mainly Norfolk, "Blow the Man Down": a halyard shanty, the crew resting on the rope through the shantyman's line, pulling on the refrain]; song facts [The Traditional Ballad Index, "Blow the Man Down": earliest trace July 1867, the Syracuse Daily Courier; Hugill's six versions]; credits: words and tune traditional; licence.source: W. B. Whall, Ships, Sea Songs and Shanties (3rd ed., James Brown & Son, Glasgow, 1913), p. 92, printed in D in 3/8, written here in F in 3/4, archive.org/details/shipsseasongssha00whal; decisions.md row (Whall's "Way-ay", the calls solo); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'blow-the-man-down' => 'Blow the Man Down is a halyard shanty, sung while hoisting heavy yards: the crew rested on the rope while the shantyman sang his line, then took a big pull together on the refrain. The Traditional Ballad Index gives its earliest known date as July 1867, in a Syracuse newspaper, and the shanty collector Stan Hugill later sorted it into six separate versions. Our arrangement is read from W. B. Whall\'s Ships, Sea Songs and Shanties (third edition, 1913), keeping his "Way-ay", and moved from D up to F. This is three verses for 2 to 4 singers, brisk and swinging: the soprano is the shantyman and sings every call alone, and everyone hauls in on the answers in harmony.',
    // Sources: song facts [Cecil J. Sharp, English Folk-Chanteys (1914), No. XXVII and its note, read from the scan: John Short of Watchet; the dorian mode where Tozer, Bullen and Whall print major]; credits: words and tune traditional (as sung by John Short, Watchet; printed 1914); licence.source: Sharp, English Folk-Chanteys (London and Taunton, 1914), No. XXVII, p. 32, in C dorian, written here in C# dorian, three of his verses, archive.org/details/englishfolkchant00shar; decisions.md row 2026-11-02 (chorus, three verses, chorus; the soprano the shantyman); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'haul-away-joe' => 'Haul Away, Joe is a pulling shanty: the shantyman sings a line alone, and everyone answers "Way, haul away, haul away, Joe", landing together on "Joe". Our arrangement is read from Cecil Sharp\'s English Folk-Chanteys of 1914: the tune John Short, an old sailor from the harbour town of Watchet, sang for it, with the harmony of Sharp\'s piano part. Tozer, Bullen and Whall print the shanty in a major key, but Sharp notes that Short\'s tune is in the dorian mode, which gives it a darker sound, and we keep it, a semitone up. This is the chorus, three verses and the chorus again, for 2 to 4 singers, the soprano singing each call alone.',
    // Sources: song facts [Sōkyokushū (Ministry of Education, Tokyo, 1888), No. 2, read from the scan: the first printing, for the koto, the tune in Western notes; the five-note in scale and its open ending]; credits: words and tune traditional Japanese (printed 1888); licence.source: Rudolf Dittrich, Nippon Gakufu (Leipzig: Breitkopf & Härtel, Copyright 1894), No. 3, Sakura, in D minor, written here a whole tone lower; the syllable fitting from the Sōkyokushū (Tokyo, 1888), No. 2, consulted, dl.ndl.go.jp/pid/857651; decisions.md rows 2026-11-01 (sung in Japanese, romaji in the lane; held notes on "oo", no Western chords) and 2026-11-08 (the held parts breathe apart, Hillel); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'sakura-sakura' => 'Sakura Sakura, the Japanese song of cherry blossom, has its earliest known printing in 1888, by Tokyo\'s music school, in a collection of music for the koto, a long zither, with the tune written in Western notes. It uses only five notes, the in scale of Japanese koto music, and ends on a note that sounds open rather than finished. Our arrangement is read from that 1888 printing, the Sōkyokushū, and moved a minor third up to sit in a beginner\'s range. It is sung in Japanese, with the words shown in romaji, slow and calm, for 2 to 4 singers: the tune over soft held notes on "oo", like a koto\'s open strings, with no Western chords.',
    // Sources: song facts [Boletín bibliográfico español, vol. 2 (Madrid, 1861), p. 122, entry 917, read from the scan: "canción americana", in print in Madrid by 1859; La Paloma (Century Music Publishing, 1906), read from the scan: the words beginning as a man leaves Havana; the piano's habanera rhythm throughout]; credits: words Sebastián Iradier, as usually credited (the 1859 Madrid edition names him for the music), tune Sebastián Iradier (published in Madrid, 1859); licence.source: La Paloma, canción americana (Century Music Publishing Co., New York, 1906, plate 84-4), pp. 2 to 5, in C, checked against Artists' Vocal Album (White, Smith & Co., Boston, 1887), pp. 66 to 69, archive.org/details/lapalomadovecanc00yrad; decisions.md row 2026-11-02 (verse 1 and the refrain in Spanish; the bass's habanera on "dm"); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'la-paloma' => 'La Paloma is Sebastián Iradier\'s habanera, in print in Madrid by 1859, which he called a "canción americana", an American song. Its words begin as a man leaves Havana. Our arrangement is read from a 1906 New York edition, checked against a Boston printing of 1887, in C as printed. This is the first verse and the refrain, sung in Spanish, for 2 to 4 singers. Under the tune the piano of the print plays the habanera rhythm all the way through, a long note, a short one, then two even ones, and here the bass sings it on "dm", while the alto and tenor sing the words.',
    // Sources: song facts [Mozart's Werke, Serie 3, No. 31 (Breitkopf & Härtel, 1880), read from the scan: 1791, the last year of his life, K. 618; Adagio and sotto voce]; credits: words a 13th-century Eucharistic hymn, music Mozart (1791); licence.source: Mozart's Werke, Serie 3, No. 31, K. 618, pp. 123 to 124 (Leipzig, 1880), in D, the voices only, IMSLP #78492; decisions.md row 2026-11-01 (4 singers only; the strings' interlude a bar's rest); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'ave-verum-corpus' => 'Ave verum corpus is a short motet Mozart wrote in 1791, the last year of his life, catalogued as K. 618, setting a medieval Latin hymn. He marked it Adagio and sotto voce, "under the voice": slow, and soft all the way through, without one loud note. Our arrangement is read from the 1880 Breitkopf & Härtel edition of Mozart\'s works, in D as he wrote it, with his own four voice parts and the Latin words. The strings\' introduction and ending are left out, and their short interlude becomes a bar\'s rest. This is for 4 singers only, as the chords need all four of Mozart\'s voices.',
    // Sources: song facts [Morley, A Plaine and Easie Introduction to Practicall Musicke (1597), excerpt on a University of Mississippi course page: balletts, or fa las, for dancing as well as singing]; song facts [IMSLP, "Balletts to Five Voyces, Book 1 (Morley, Thomas)": two 1595 editions from Thomas Este, English and Italian]; credits: words anonymous (printed 1595), music Thomas Morley (1595); licence.source: Morley's own part-books, First Booke of Balletts (London: Este, 1595), No. III, Fellowes's English Madrigal School, vol. IV (1913), consulted only; decisions.md row 2026-11-01 (Hillel: "4 singers only"; the two tenors joined); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'now-is-the-month-of-maying' => 'Now Is the Month of Maying is a ballett by Thomas Morley, printed in London in 1595, its English words by an unknown poet. In his music textbook of 1597, Morley said balletts, also called fa las, were songs for dancing as well as singing. His 1595 book of them came out in two editions, one in English and one with Italian words, where this song takes its words from a song by Orazio Vecchi. Our arrangement is read from E. H. Fellowes\'s 1913 edition, its accidentals checked against the 1595 part-books, with Morley\'s two tenor parts joined into one. This is verse 1 and its "fa la" refrains, each half sung twice, for 4 singers, light and dancing, every voice with a tune of its own.',
    // Sources: song facts ["Asleep in the Deep" (New York: F. A. Mills), Lester S. Levy Sheet Music Collection, Johns Hopkins University, Box 145, Item 046, read from the scan: written for a baritone or bass voice; the last line sung twice, its last "beware!" an octave below the first]; credits: words Arthur J. Lamb, music H. W. Petrie (1897); licence.source: the F. A. Mills printing, in F, checked against M. Witmark & Sons' edition in D (IMSLP 191240), verse 1 and the refrain; decisions.md row 2026-11-02 (the bass sings the tune; the others "oo" on Petrie's harmony); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'asleep-in-the-deep' => 'Asleep in the Deep is a sea song of 1897, with words by Arthur J. Lamb and music by H. W. Petrie, who wrote it for a baritone or bass voice. That is why here the bass, not the soprano, sings the tune. Its last line keeps its lowest notes for the end: it is sung twice, the second time sinking through "asleep in the deep" to a last "beware!" a whole octave below the first. Our arrangement is read from a New York printing by F. A. Mills, checked against Witmark\'s edition. This is the verse and the refrain for 2 to 4 singers, rolling and dramatic, the other parts holding soft chords on "oo" on Petrie\'s harmony.',
    // Sources: song facts [The Pirates of Penzance, vocal score (Chappell), Act II No. 12, read from the scan: Allegro marziale, louder as it goes; two choruses at once, pirates and police]; credits: words W. S. Gilbert, music Arthur Sullivan (1879); licence.source: the Chappell vocal score (plate 23731), Act II No. 12, pp. 151 to 152, in D for men's double chorus, written here in A, archive.org/details/vocalscoreofpira00sull; decisions.md row 2026-11-02 (the women take the pirates, the men the police); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'with-cat-like-tread' => 'With Cat-like Tread is the pirates\' stealthy march from Gilbert and Sullivan\'s The Pirates of Penzance of 1879. The pirates sing about creeping in silence, but Sullivan marks their chorus Allegro marziale, like a march, and it gets louder as it goes: the joke is in the music. Our arrangement is read from Chappell\'s vocal score, the end of the Act II reprise, where two choruses sing at once, the pirates creeping along while the police answer "Tarantara!". Sullivan wrote it for men. Here, as mixed choirs sing it, the soprano and alto take the pirates and the tenor and bass the police, moved from D to A.',
    // Sources: song facts [the song facts' sources, checked by the courses lead: Henry Francis Lyte, Remains (1850), memoir pp. l to lvi and the hymn signed "Berry Head, September, 1847"; and Chris Fenner, Abide with me, Hymnology Archive; also Canterbury Dictionary of Hymnology, "Henry Francis Lyte"; Cardiff City FC, "90 years: a final of firsts" (2017); and Barry Richards, Bournemouth University ("introduced at the 1927 final"); also Public Domain Review, "Abide With Me (1914)"]; credits: words Henry Francis Lyte (1847), tune William Henry Monk, "Eventide" (1861); licence.source: Hymns Ancient and Modern (Novello, 1861), Hymn 14, in E-flat, checked against the 1875 edition, Hymn 27, archive.org/details/hymnsancientmode00unse; decisions.md row 2026-11-08 (verse 1, every line one breath; Monk's chromatic alto kept); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'abide-with-me' => 'Abide with Me is Henry Francis Lyte\'s evening hymn, which he wrote in 1847, in failing health, shortly before he left England for the warmer south; he died at Nice that November. Its tune, Eventide, is by William Henry Monk, who printed it in Hymns Ancient and Modern in 1861. It has been sung before the FA Cup Final in England since 1927. Our arrangement is read from that 1861 hymnal, in Monk\'s own four parts in E-flat, checked against the 1875 edition. This is the first verse for 2 to 4 singers, slow and warm, every line one breath.',
    // Sources: song facts [the song facts' sources, checked by the courses lead: Hymnary.org, "Shall We Gather at the River?", text and tune notes; Chris Fenner, "Shall we gather at the river", Hymnology Archive (2018, revised 2025); Happy Voices (New York: American Tract Society, 1865), No. 220; BBC Music Magazine, "Shall We Gather at the River lyrics", classical-music.com (2022); Tim Dirks, "Stagecoach (1939)", Filmsite; Craig Lysy, reviews of the Stagecoach and The Searchers scores, Movie Music UK (2024, 2017)]; credits: words and music Robert Lowry (1864); licence.source: Lowry, Bright Jewels for the Sunday School (Biglow & Main, 1869), p. 110, "Beautiful River", in E-flat, archive.org/details/brightjewelsfors00lowr_0; decisions.md row 2026-11-08 (verse 1 and the chorus; the tenor and bass high in the chorus); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'shall-we-gather' => 'Shall We Gather at the River is Robert Lowry\'s hymn of 1864, words and music both his. Its earliest known printing, in 1865, called it Beautiful River, and its tune was later named Hanson Place, after the Brooklyn Baptist church where he was pastor when he wrote it. The film director John Ford used it in many of his westerns, Stagecoach and The Searchers among them, and the critic Roger Ebert called it Ford\'s favourite hymn. Our arrangement is read from the four parts Lowry printed in his Bright Jewels for the Sunday School of 1869, in E-flat as printed. This is the first verse and the chorus for 2 to 4 singers, cheerful and steady.',
    // Sources: song facts [the song facts' sources, checked by the courses lead: Hymnology Archive, "Come, thou fount of every blessing"; and Canterbury Dictionary of Hymnology, "Come, thou fount of every blessing"; also Hymnary.org (born 27 September 1735); Hymnology Archive, "Come, thou fount of every blessing"; and Psalter Hymnal Handbook at Hymnary.org, NETTLETON]; credits: words Robert Robinson (1758), tune "Nettleton" (1813); licence.source: The Methodist Hymnal (Eaton & Mains, 1905), No. 19, "Nettleton", in E-flat, archive.org/details/methodisthymnalo00meth; decisions.md row 2026-11-08 (verse 1, a breath at every line); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'come-thou-fount' => 'Come, Thou Fount of Every Blessing has words by Robert Robinson, who wrote them in his early twenties; his own list of his writings dates the hymn to 1758. Its tune, Nettleton, has its earliest known printing in John Wyeth\'s Repository of Sacred Music, Part Second, in 1813. It is named after the preacher Asahel Nettleton, and some books credit him with it, but who wrote it is not known. Our arrangement is read from the four parts The Methodist Hymnal printed in 1905, in E-flat. This is the first verse for 2 to 4 singers, bright and lilting in three, every line one breath.',
    // Sources: song facts [the song facts' sources, checked by the courses lead: Canterbury Dictionary of Hymnology, "Nearer, my God, to thee" (Canterbury Press); Hymnary.org, "Nearer, My God, to Thee", text notes; Archibald Gracie, The Truth about the Titanic (New York: Mitchell Kennerley, 1913), ch. 1; NPR Morning Edition, "Remembering The Titanic\'s Intrepid Bandleader" (13 April 2012)]; credits: words Sarah Flower Adams (1841), tune Lowell Mason, "Bethany" (1856); licence.source: The Sabbath Hymn and Tune Book, ed. Mason, Park and Phelps (Mason Brothers, 1859), p. 244, "Bethany", in G, archive.org/details/sabbtune00maso; decisions.md row 2026-11-08 (verse 1, every line one breath); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'nearer-my-god' => 'Nearer, My God, to Thee has words by Sarah Flower Adams, who wrote them for the hymn book of William Johnson Fox\'s chapel in London, in print by 1841, retelling Jacob\'s dream in Genesis 28, of a ladder between earth and heaven. Some Titanic survivors said the ship\'s band played it as she sank; others denied it, so no one knows for sure. Its tune, Bethany, is by Lowell Mason, and our arrangement is read from his own four parts in The Sabbath Hymn and Tune Book of 1859, in G as printed. This is the first verse for 2 to 4 singers, calm and even, every line one breath.',
    // Sources: song facts [the song facts' sources, checked by the courses lead: Folger Shakespeare, The Merry Wives of Windsor 5.5 (FTLN 2675-2676); and The Complete Works of William Shakespeare (MIT), Merry Wives 2.1 and 5.5; The Oxford Book of Carols (1928), No. 28, "The Old Year Now Away Is Fled", from New Christmas Carols (1642) "to the tune of Greensleeves"; and Douglas D. Anderson, Hymns and Carols of Christmas, "What Child Is This" (citing Keyte and Parrott, New Oxford Book of Carols)]; credits: words and tune traditional English, 16th century; licence.source: William Chappell, Popular Music of the Olden Time, vol. 1 (Cramer, Beale & Chappell, 1855 to 1859), p. 230, with G. A. Macfarren's harmony, in E minor, written here a whole tone higher, archive.org/details/popularmusicofol01chapuoft; decisions.md row 2026-11-08 (verse 1 and the refrain; Macfarren's bass, our alto and tenor); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'greensleeves' => 'Greensleeves is an English tune of the 1500s, sung here in its own old words, "Alas, my love, you do me wrong". Shakespeare names it twice in The Merry Wives of Windsor, where Falstaff cries, "Let the sky rain potatoes, let it thunder to the tune of Greensleeves." People were singing Christmas-season words to it by the 1600s; the best known today, "What Child Is This?", are by William Chatterton Dix, in print by 1867. Our arrangement is read from William Chappell\'s Popular Music of the Olden Time of the 1850s, on the harmony G. A. Macfarren wrote for it, a whole tone higher. This is the first verse and the refrain for 2 to 4 singers, gentle and lilting.',
    // Sources: song facts [the song facts' sources, checked by the courses lead: Jeremy Dibble, "Jerusalem: A Hymn to Women\'s Suffrage", Culture Matters (2016); and "Jerusalem the Golden", Standpoint (April 2013), quoting Parry\'s diary for 10 March 1916; Jason Whittaker, Prospect (26 December 2019); Jeremy Dibble, "Jerusalem: A Hymn to Women\'s Suffrage", Culture Matters (2016); Jason Whittaker, Prospect (2019); "Jerusalem the Golden", Standpoint (2013)]; credits: words William Blake (c. 1804), from the preface to Milton, tune Hubert Parry (1916); licence.source: Parry's unison song (J. Curwen & Sons, 1916), read from Curwen's plate in The Old Country (Dent, 1917), in D, checked against The Hymnary (Toronto, 1930), No. 522, with Songs of Praise (OUP, 1925), No. 204, consulted only; decisions.md row 2026-11-08 (Parry's tune and left hand, our alto and tenor); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'jerusalem' => 'Jerusalem sets William Blake\'s "And did those feet in ancient time", from the preface to his Milton of about 1804, to music Hubert Parry wrote on 10 March 1916, at the request of the poet Robert Bridges for the Fight for Right movement in London. Campaigners for women\'s votes took it up in 1918, and Parry wrote to their leader, Millicent Fawcett, that he wished it might become the Women Voters\' hymn. He wrote it as a unison song with accompaniment: here the tune is his, the bass follows his left hand, and the alto and tenor are ours on his harmony. This is the first verse, broad and steady, in D, for 2 to 4 singers.',
    // Sources: song facts [the song facts' sources, checked by the courses lead: NAACP, "Lift Every Voice and Sing", History Explained; Chris Fenner, "Lift every voice and sing", Hymnology Archive (2019), quoting Johnson\'s own account; Cait Miller, Library of Congress In the Muse blog (8 February 2022); Cait Miller, "Lift Every Voice and Sing", Library of Congress In the Muse blog (2022); Burton W. Peretti, "Lift Ev\'ry Voice and Sing", National Recording Registry essay, Library of Congress]; credits: words James Weldon Johnson, music J. Rosamond Johnson (1900); licence.source: Rosamond Johnson's own setting (Jos. W. Stern & Co., New York, 1900, plate 3071-3), a semitone lower; decisions.md row 2026-11-08 (his four parts smoothed for beginners; the lower parts on his organ's chords where every voice sings the tune); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'lift-every-voice' => 'Lift Every Voice and Sing has words by James Weldon Johnson and music by his brother, J. Rosamond Johnson. James wrote the poem for Lincoln\'s birthday in Jacksonville, Florida, in 1900, where 500 schoolchildren first sang it. By 1920 the NAACP was calling it the "Negro national anthem". Our arrangement is read from Rosamond Johnson\'s own four voice parts in the earliest known printing, by Jos. W. Stern of New York in 1900, a semitone lower and smoothed for beginners. Where he has every voice sing "Sing a song full of the faith" in octaves, the lower parts hold his organ\'s chords under the tune. This is the first verse for 2 to 4 singers, broad and stately.',
    // Sources: song facts [W. B. Whall, Ships, Sea Songs and Shanties (3rd ed., 1913), p. 107: sung by the whole crew at a quick march, walking away with the rope; dropped when crews got smaller]; song facts [R. R. Terry, The Shanty Book, Part I (1921), introduction: one of the shanties in the old modes]; credits: words and tune traditional (as printed by Terry, 1921); licence.source: Terry, The Shanty Book, Part I (J. Curwen & Sons, 1921), No. 14, pp. 30 and 31, in D dorian, written here a whole tone higher, archive.org/details/shantybooksailor00terr; decisions.md row 2026-11-08 (the soprano the shantyman; the chorus on Terry's harmony); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'drunken-sailor' => 'Drunken Sailor is the shanty everyone knows. The sea captain W. B. Whall wrote that the whole crew sang it together at a quick march, walking away with the rope, and that it dropped out of use when crews got too small to walk away with anything. Richard Terry, who printed this tune in 1921, named it as one of the shanties in the old modes, not a modern major or minor key. Our arrangement is read from Terry\'s book, on the harmony of his piano part, a whole tone higher. This is the verse and the chorus for 2 to 4 singers: the soprano sings the verse alone, and everyone comes in on "Hooray and up she rises".',
    // Sources: song facts [Cecil J. Sharp, English Folk-Chanteys (1914), note to No. 2: sung coming into port, the shantyman saying what the crew thought of the ship]; song facts [Sharp (1914), contents, introduction and note to No. 2: among the capstan shanties; this version the usual form]; credits: words and tune traditional (John Short's version, as noted by Sharp, 1914); licence.source: Sharp, English Folk-Chanteys (London and Taunton, 1914), No. II, first version, p. 3, note p. 65, in E dorian, verses 1 to 3 of seven, archive.org/details/englishfolkchant00shar; decisions.md row 2026-11-08 (the soprano the shantyman; the chorus on Sharp's harmony); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'leave-her-johnny' => 'Leave Her, Johnny is a homeward-bound shanty. Sailors sang it as the voyage ended, as the ship came into port or once she was tied up, and the shantyman used it to say what the crew thought of the ship they were about to leave. Cecil Sharp put it among the capstan shanties, for steady work like marching round the capstan or working the pumps, and called this version the usual form of the tune. Our arrangement is read from his English Folk-Chanteys of 1914, as John Short of Watchet sang it, in E dorian as printed, on the harmony of Sharp\'s piano part. This is three verses for 2 to 4 singers, the soprano singing each grumble alone.',
    // Sources: song facts [the song facts' sources, checked by the courses lead: IMSLP, Caro mio ben (Giordani, Tommaso), the London print by John Preston (ca. 1785); and Katie Buzard, Illinois Public Media, Clef Notes (2024); also Art Song Central, Giordani: Caro mio ben; Todd Edward Ranney, Vocal Preparation for the High School Male, DMA document, Ohio State University (2009); and Katie Buzard, Illinois Public Media (2024)]; credits: words anonymous (18th century), music attributed to Giuseppe Giordani, probably Tommaso Giordani; licence.source: Anthology of Italian Song of the 17th and 18th Centuries, ed. Alessandro Parisotti (G. Schirmer, 1894), pp. 131 to 133, archive.org/details/anthologyofitali00pari; decisions.md rows 2026-11-08 (sung in Italian, the lower parts humming his piano harmony; the hummed parts breathe apart, Hillel); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'caro-mio-ben' => 'Caro mio ben is an Italian love song of the late 1700s, often the first art song a voice teacher gives a new student: slow, gentle and in a comfortable range. Alessandro Parisotti\'s Anthology of Italian Song of 1894 credits it to Giuseppe Giordani, and many books still do, but later research points to Tommaso Giordani: an early London print, of about 1785, says the singer Pacchierotti sang it at a concert there. Our arrangement is read from Parisotti\'s anthology, in E-flat: the soprano sings his voice line in Italian, and the alto, tenor and bass hum the harmony of his piano part on "oo". It is slow and tender, with time to breathe, for 1 to 4 singers.',
    // Sources: song facts [the song facts' sources, checked by the courses lead: Shorty George Blues (Chicago: Geo. W. Thomas Music Co., 1924), title page; James Head, "Thomas, Hociel", Handbook of Texas Online, Texas State Historical Association (2006, revised 2017); Bill Edwards, "George Washington Thomas", RagPiano.com; Shorty George Blues (Chicago: Geo. W. Thomas Music Co., copyright 1924 by Geo. W. Thomas); Bill Edwards, "George Washington Thomas", RagPiano.com; "George and Hersal Thomas", San Diego Ragtime essay]; song facts; credits: words George W. Thomas and Hociel Thomas, music George W. Thomas (1924); licence.source: Shorty George Blues (Geo. W. Thomas Music Co., Chicago, 1924), stamped "MAR 14 1924", in F, written here a whole tone higher, five cleared verses; decisions.md rows 2026-11-08 (the lower parts hum his piano's chords; the hummed parts breathe apart, Hillel); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'shorty-george-blues' => 'Shorty George Blues is a slow twelve-bar blues of 1924, with music by George W. Thomas and words by him and his daughter, Hociel Thomas. George ran his own music publishing company in Chicago and printed this song himself. Hociel became a blues singer and pianist herself, and made records in the mid-1920s. Our arrangement is read from that 1924 printing, a whole tone higher, in five verses. The soprano sings the tune with its blue notes, and the alto, tenor and bass hum the chords of his piano part underneath, for 1 to 4 singers.',
    // Sources: [the usual account, 3 December 1847, Mâcon to Dijon, for Midnight Mass in Roquemaure: a later Roquemaure parish priest's history, as Denis Havard de la Montagne, "Minuit Chrétiens, une partition d'Adolphe Adam", Musica et Memoria (musimem.com/adam.htm), follows it; The Canterbury Dictionary of Hymnology, "Minuit, chrétiens" (2013)]; credits [Dwight's English words of 1855]; [the courses lead's story check: Chansroux's account of 1895 dates the words to 1843; Dwight's Journal, 30 November 1861, and G. W. Cooke, John Sullivan Dwight (1898), on his opposition to slavery]; credits: words Placide Cappeau (1847), English John Sullivan Dwight (1855), music Adolphe Adam (1847); licence.source: Carols Old and Carols New, ed. Charles L. Hutchins (Boston: The Parish Choir, 1916), Carol 741, pp. 617 to 620, in E-flat, written here a minor third lower; decisions.md row 2026-11-08 (the refrain's choir parts, the verse on the organ's harmony); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'o-holy-night' => 'O Holy Night is Adolphe Adam\'s Christmas song, "Minuit, chrétiens", to words by Placide Cappeau of Roquemaure. The usual account dates the words to December 1847, written on a journey from Mâcon to Dijon for Midnight Mass in Roquemaure; an account printed in 1895 puts them in 1843. The English words are by John Sullivan Dwight, the American critic and minister, who wrote them in 1855 and opposed slavery in his own writings. Our arrangement is read from Carols Old and Carols New, a Boston carol book of 1916, a minor third lower: its choir parts in the refrain, "Fall on your knees", and quiet chords on its organ harmony in the verse.',
    // Sources: [the song facts' sources, checked by the courses lead: J. B. T. Marsh, The Story of the Jubilee Singers, with Their Songs, second edition (London: Hodder and Stoughton, 1875), No. 77, pp. 196 to 197 (Universitätsbibliothek Gießen, Gb 4660), and the sixth edition (1876), p. 196; and University of Pittsburgh, Voices Across Time, "Deep River"]; [Library of Congress, Performing Arts Encyclopedia, "H. T. Burleigh (1866-1949)", loc.gov/item/ihas.200035730: baritone and composer; Hymnology Archive, "Harry T Burleigh": the singer and composer Harry T. Burleigh]; licence.source: Burleigh's octavo (G. Schirmer, 8vo Choruses No. 5815, "Copyright, 1914, by G. Schirmer"), for mixed voices, in E-flat (an earlier 1913 printing is reported, so the text says what our copy is); licence.arrangement and decisions.md row 2026-11-10 (one line where the tenors or basses divide; Bass II's E-flat 2 an octave up in bars 8 and 27; 4 only); song file (the alto's tune twice, the soprano's entry at bar 9, the verse "Oh, don't you want to go")
    'deep-river' => 'Deep River is an anonymous African-American spiritual, in print by 1875, in J. B. T. Marsh\'s The Story of the Jubilee Singers, and popularised by the singer and composer Harry T. Burleigh. This is Burleigh\'s own setting for mixed chorus, as G. Schirmer printed it in 1914, in E-flat as he wrote it: where his tenors or basses divide we sing one line, and two low bass notes go up an octave. It is for 4 singers, slow and smooth: the alto sings the tune twice, the soprano joining the second time, on long notes above it, then everyone sings the verse, "Oh, don\'t you want to go".',
    // Sources: song facts [Hollis Dann Music Course, Fourth Year Music (American Book Company, 1916), p. 115, read from the scan: "May Bells", a round for two voices, II at bar 5]; song facts [Traditional Ballad Index, "White Coral Bells": earliest date 1916, the book names no author, nor for any of its other rounds (pp. 32, 47, 92, 101, 106, 109 and 125, read from the scan)]; credits: words and tune anonymous (printed 1916); licence.source: E-flat, written here a fourth lower in B-flat, up to four voices entering four bars apart; decisions.md row 2026-11-10 (both verses twice, the last note held to the bar line); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'white-coral-bells' => 'White Coral Bells is an American round about lilies of the valley. Its earliest known printing is "May Bells", in Hollis Dann\'s Fourth Year Music, a school music reader of 1916, which names no author for it, nor for any of its other rounds. There it is a round for two voices. Our round is read from that 1916 page, with its words, "deck our garden walk" and "That could happen only when the fairies sing", a fourth lower, in B-flat. Here it is for 2 to 4 singers: everyone sings both verses twice, entering four bars apart, with the tenor and bass an octave lower, and its four lines sound together, one in each voice.',
    // Sources: song facts ["Daily Express" Community Song Book, ed. John Goss (London, 1927), p. 195, read from the scan: its four lines numbered for the voices' entries]; song facts [David Vinton, The Masonick Minstrel (Dedham, Massachusetts, 1816), p. 21, read from the scan: "Scotland's Burning! Canon." in four numbered lines, "Look out"]; credits: words traditional, the line "Fetch the engines" first found in Goss (1927), its maker unknown; tune traditional; licence.source: the line ends numbered 4, 3, 2, 1, read as where each voice stops so all finish together, in G, written here a whole tone lower in F; the 1927 book's note signed "John Goss, Lancaster Gate, February, 1927", and "A New Community Song Book", Musical Courier 97, no. 23 (6 December 1928) (the baritone John Goss edited and compiled the book), with The Canadian Encyclopedia, "John Goss" (died 1953); decisions.md row 2026-11-10 (three times through, everyone stopping together); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'londons-burning' => 'London\'s Burning is an English round about a fire and the call to put it out; a version called "Scotland\'s Burning" was printed in America in 1816. Our round is read from the Daily Express Community Song Book of 1927, edited by the baritone John Goss, a whole tone lower, in F. Goss numbers its four lines for the voices to come in, and numbers their ends 4, 3, 2, 1, which we read as where each voice stops, so that everyone finishes together. Here it is for 2 to 4 singers: the soprano sings it three times, each voice entering two bars after the last, the tenor and bass an octave lower, and all stop together on one chord.',
    // Sources: song facts [Notes and Queries, 7th series, vol. 2 (25 December 1886), p. 507, read from the scan: "the school round", the words in all the copies of the round he had met, "They all ran after the farmer's wife"]; song facts [Home Songs (Oliver Ditson Company, Boston, 1906), p. 95, read from the scan: a collection of favourite songs, hymns and rounds "for the fireside", "Three Blind Mice (round in four parts)"]; credits: words and tune traditional; licence.source: in D, written here a whole tone lower in C, entries four bars apart; decisions.md row 2026-11-10 (twice through, the 1906 words); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'three-blind-mice' => 'Three Blind Mice is the nursery round almost everyone knows. In 1886 a reader of Notes and Queries called it "the school round" and quoted the words in all the copies he had met, "They all ran after the farmer\'s wife". Our round is read from Home Songs, a Boston book of 1906 of songs, hymns and rounds, which prints it in four parts, with its words "She cut off their tails" and "Did ever you see such a sight", a whole tone lower, in C. Here it is for 2 to 4 singers: everyone sings it twice, entering four bars apart, with the tenor and bass an octave lower, and the parts tumble over each other.',
    // Sources: song facts [Theodor Fliedner, ed., Lieder-Buch für Kleinkinder-Schulen (Kaiserswerth, 1842), p. 29, No. 54, read from the scan: no composer named; Lieder-Projekt (Carus-Verlag and SWR2), "O wie wohl ist mir am Abend": a folk round after the canon "Ich bin fröhlich" by Carl Friedrich Schulz; Der Große Brockhaus, vol. 14 (Leipzig, copyright 1933), p. 27, read from the scan: credited instead to J. A. Schulz]; song facts [H. F. Sefton, Three-Part Songs (Toronto, 1869), p. 41, read from the scan: an earlier English version, "When to rest the bells are ringing"]; credits: English words anonymous (printed 1896); licence.source: The Educational Music Course, First Reader (Ginn & Company, Boston, 1896), p. 22, "Round" in three parts six bars apart, in F, written here a whole tone lower in E-flat; decisions.md row 2026-11-10 (three times through); song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'oh-how-lovely' => 'Oh, How Lovely Is the Evening is a round about evening bells. It comes from a German round, "O wie wohl ist mir am Abend", which a German songbook series today calls a folk round after a canon by Karl Friedrich Schulz. Our round is read from the First Reader of The Educational Music Course, a Boston school music book of 1896, which prints it as a round in three parts in F; we sing it a whole tone lower, in E-flat. Its third line is the bells themselves, "Ding, dong" on one low note. Here it is for 2 to 4 singers: everyone sings it three times, entering six bars apart, with the tenor and bass an octave lower.',
    // Sources: song facts [Dett, ed., Religious Folk-Songs of the Negro as Sung at Hampton Institute (1927), pp. 60 and 61, read from the scan: the refrain marked "Tutti", the first line of each verse "Solo quasi extempore sed meme ritmo", the second "Solo capriccioso"]; song facts [the same, p. 60: among the "Hymns on Biblical Themes", marked "Poco allegro ritmo marcato"]; credits: words and tune traditional spiritual, as sung at Hampton Institute (printed 1909), arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: original, the printed four-part setting used only to check the chords; licence.source: the 1909 New Edition (Hampton), pp. 164 to 165, the same setting as Dett (1927), pp. 60 to 61, in A-flat and written here a semitone down in G; the refrain, then verses 1 and 2 of the three printed, each followed by the refrain; description: the soprano sings each verse line alone, everyone answers "'Way in de middle ob de air"; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'ezekiel-saw-the-wheel' => 'Ezekiel Saw the Wheel is a spiritual as Hampton Institute sang it. Its 1927 songbook, edited by R. Nathaniel Dett, files it among the "Hymns on Biblical Themes", fairly lively, with a firm, marked rhythm. The 1927 book marks the refrain "Tutti", for everyone, and the first line of each verse "Solo quasi extempore": for one voice, as if made up on the spot. Our tune and words are read from the school\'s 1909 songbook, whose setting the 1927 book reprints, a semitone lower, in G; the four parts are our own, checked against its chords. This is the refrain and two of its three verses for 2 to 4 singers: the soprano sings each verse\'s solo lines alone, and everyone answers "\'Way in de middle ob de air".',
    // Sources: song facts [Dett, ed., Religious Folk-Songs of the Negro as Sung at Hampton Institute (1927), introduction, section IX, p. xviii: tunes and harmonies set down as the students sang them, in a tradition going back to 1868]; credits: words and tune traditional spiritual, as sung at Hampton Institute (printed 1909), arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: original, the printed four-part refrain used only to check the chords; licence.source: the 1909 New Edition (Hampton), p. 169, "Ev'ry Time I Feel the Spirit", the same setting as Dett (1927), p. 169, in E-flat as printed and as written here; the refrain twice as its repeat marks say, then verses 1 and 2 of the three printed, each followed by the refrain twice; the solo verse sung by the alto; description: at an easy walking pace; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'every-time-i-feel-the-spirit' => 'Every Time I Feel the Spirit is a spiritual as Hampton Institute sang it. R. Nathaniel Dett, who edited the school\'s songbook in 1927, wrote that its tunes and harmonies were set down as the students sang them, in a tradition going back to 1868. Our tune and words are read from the school\'s 1909 songbook, whose setting the 1927 book reprints, where it is called Ev\'ry Time I Feel the Spirit, in E-flat as printed; the four parts are our own, checked against its chords. This is the refrain, sung twice as its repeat marks say, and two of its three verses, each followed by the refrain, for 2 to 4 singers, at an easy walking pace: the alto sings each verse alone.',
    // Sources: song facts [Dett, ed., Religious Folk-Songs of the Negro as Sung at Hampton Institute (1927), p. 104, read from the scan: the related "Did You Hear How Dey Crucified My Lord?"; "Were You There" seemed "to be universally more popular"]; credits: words and tune traditional spiritual, as sung at Hampton Institute (published 1909), arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: original, the printed four-part setting used only to check the chords; licence.source: the 1909 New Edition (Hampton), p. 162, which Dett (1927), Appendix p. VI, reprints, in G as printed and as written here, verses 1 to 4 of the 5; description: the soprano asks each question alone, the others echo it under her held note, slow and quiet; song file (the parts' guide notes: the echo is "were you there?" in verses 1 and 4, "to the tree?" and "in the side?" in verses 2 and 3)
    'were-you-there' => 'Were You There is a spiritual as Hampton Institute\'s singers sang it. The school\'s songbook, edited by R. Nathaniel Dett in 1927, also prints a related spiritual, "Did You Hear How Dey Crucified My Lord?", and notes that this one seemed "to be universally more popular". Our tune and words are read from the school\'s 1909 songbook, whose setting the 1927 book reprints, in G as printed; the four parts are our own, checked against its chords. This is four of its five verses for 2 to 4 singers, slow and quiet: the soprano asks each verse\'s question alone, the others echo her under her held note, and everyone sings the rest together.',
    // Sources: song facts [Seward, Jubilee Songs (Biglow & Main, 1872), preface, p. 3, and pp. 16 and 17: more than half the Fisk melodies without the fourth and seventh; this tune uses only five notes]; credits: words and tune traditional spiritual, as sung by the Fisk Jubilee Singers (published 1872), arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: original (the source prints the tune alone, "Sung in Unison"); licence.source: Seward (1872), pp. 16 to 17, in G minor, written here a tone up in A minor; the refrain, then verses 1, 2 and 4 of the 4, each into the refrain; description: the soprano sings each verse alone, everyone joins its last line, steady and rhythmic; song file (the parts' guide notes: the verses' last lines are "And why not every man?", "King Jesus shall be mine." and "And I'll never come back any more.")
    'didnt-my-lord-deliver-daniel' => 'Didn\'t My Lord Deliver Daniel is a spiritual from the Fisk Jubilee Singers\' book of 1872, Theodore F. Seward\'s Jubilee Songs. Seward noted that more than half the book\'s melodies leave out the fourth and seventh notes of the scale; this tune is one of them, using only five notes. Our tune and words are read from its unison melody, a tone higher, in A minor; the four parts are our own. This is the refrain and three of its four verses for 2 to 4 singers: the soprano sings each verse alone, and everyone joins its last line.',
    // Sources: song facts [Dett, ed., Religious Folk-Songs of the Negro as Sung at Hampton Institute (1927), p. 88, read from the scan: headed "Transcribed from the singing of the Hampton Institute students", among the "Hymns of Consolation"]; credits: words traditional spiritual, as sung by the students of Hampton Institute (printed 1927); tune as R. Nathaniel Dett transcribed it; arrangement R. Nathaniel Dett's own four parts (1927), adapted by Stackapella; licence.arrangement: Dett's four-part refrain, a whole tone lower, every pitch as printed but a few listed changes; licence.source: Dett (1927), p. 88, in B major, written here a whole tone lower in A; the refrain, verse 1, the refrain, verse 3 and the refrain (verse 2 left out for length); description: the soprano sings each verse alone, slow and very expressive; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'balm-in-gilead' => 'There Is a Balm in Gilead is a spiritual that R. Nathaniel Dett printed in 1927 in his songbook of Hampton Institute\'s spirituals, headed "Transcribed from the singing of the Hampton Institute students": the four parts were written down as the students sang them, and filed among the "Hymns of Consolation". Our arrangement is read from that page, Dett\'s own four parts, a whole tone lower, in A. It is for 2 to 4 singers, slow and very expressive: everyone sings the refrain, twice through each time, and between refrains the soprano sings a verse alone, "Sometimes I feel discouraged", then "If you cannot preach like Peter", the first and last of the three printed.',
    // Sources: song facts [Sharp, English Folk-Chanteys (1914), note to No. 35, p. 71, read from the scan: taken down from John Short of Watchet; no variants of it in his hands, and no printed version known to him]; song facts [Sharp (1914), p. 32 and pp. 40 and 41: a pulling chantey, the pulls marked with arrow-heads, two in each chorus, on "Way" and on the "bully" right after it]; credits: words and tune traditional (as sung by John Short, Watchet; printed 1914), arrangement Stackapella, on Cecil Sharp's harmony; licence.arrangement: on the harmony of Sharp's piano accompaniment; licence.source: Sharp (1914), No. XXXV, pp. 40 to 41, in G major as printed and as written here; the refrain and verse 1 as printed, verses 2 and 3 from the two lines Sharp prints after the tune; description: the soprano the shantyman, everyone answers "Way ay bully in the alley"; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'bully-in-the-alley' => 'Bully in the Alley is a pulling shanty that Cecil Sharp took down from John Short of Watchet. Sharp wrote that he had no other version of it and knew of no printed one. He marks with arrow-heads the notes the crew pulled on, two in each chorus, on "Way" and on the "bully" right after it. Our arrangement is read from his English Folk-Chanteys of 1914, in G as printed, on the harmony of his piano part, with verses 2 and 3 made from the two lines he prints after the tune. This is the refrain and three verses for 2 to 4 singers: the soprano is the shantyman, and everyone answers "Way ay bully in the alley".',
    // Sources: song facts [Sharp, English Folk-Chanteys (1914), introduction, p. xiii, and No. 37, pp. 42 and 43: the chanteyman often began by singing the refrain alone, to announce the chantey and remind the crew of the tune; this one starts that way]; song facts [Sharp (1914), introduction, p. xi: his singer more than fifty years in sailing ships, for most of them a recognised chanteyman]; credits: words and tune traditional (as sung by John Short, Watchet; printed 1914), arrangement Stackapella, on Cecil Sharp's harmony; licence.arrangement: on the harmony of Sharp's piano accompaniment; licence.source: Sharp (1914), No. XXXVII, pp. 42 to 43, in C major, written here a tone up in D; every word and section as printed (the solo introduction, two verses each with its chorus, and the full chorus); the pull on each chorus's last "haul"; description: the soprano the shantyman; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'haul-on-the-bowline' => 'Haul on the Bowline is a pulling shanty as John Short of Watchet sang it to Cecil Sharp, who wrote that his singer had spent more than fifty years in sailing ships. Sharp writes that the chanteyman often began by singing the refrain alone, to announce the chantey and remind the crew of the tune, and this one starts that way. Our arrangement is read from his English Folk-Chanteys of 1914, a tone higher, in D, on the harmony of his piano part. This is the call, two verses and the full chorus for 2 to 4 singers: the soprano is the shantyman, and everyone lands together on each answer\'s last "haul", where Sharp marks the pull.',
    // Sources: song facts [Sharp, Folk Songs from Somerset, Fifth Series (1909), note to No. 124, pp. 89 and 90, read from the scan: "a capstan chanty"; Captain Lewis of Minehead sang it as a song, not as a chanty; round the capstan it went much faster; Sharp set his harmony for the slower pace]; song facts [the same note: the old minor form; the modernized major version "far less beautiful"]; credits: words and tune traditional (as sung by Captain Lewis, Minehead; printed 1909), arrangement Stackapella, on Cecil Sharp's harmony; licence.arrangement: the soprano Sharp's voice part note for note, the other parts on the chords of his accompaniment, the last sung chord A major as in his piano postlude; licence.source: Sharp, Folk Songs from Somerset, Fifth Series (1909), No. CXXIV, pp. 62 to 64, in A minor at the printed pitch, verse 1 and the chorus; sections: Verse 1, Chorus, Chorus; song file (the parts' guide notes: the alto's C-sharp on the last "leagues.")
    'spanish-ladies' => 'Spanish Ladies is the sailors\' farewell to Spain, printed as a capstan chanty by Cecil Sharp in Folk Songs from Somerset in 1909, from the singing of Captain Lewis of Minehead. Captain Lewis sang it as a song, not as a chanty; round the capstan, Sharp notes, it went much faster, and he set his harmony for the slower pace. Sharp prints the tune in its old minor form, finding the major version sailors of his day sang "far less beautiful". Our arrangement is read from his book, in A minor, on the harmony of his piano part, ending on his postlude\'s major chord. This is the first verse and the chorus twice for 2 to 4 singers.',
    // Sources: song facts [Terry, The Shanty Book, Part I (1921), No. 15, pp. 32 and 33: a halliard shanty, for hoisting the sails; the shantyman sings each line alone and the crew answers "Blow, boys blow"]; song facts [Terry (1921), note to No. 15, p. xv: spotless decks and masts and yards that "shone like silver" the marks of a Yankee packet ship, kept that way by a terrible discipline]; credits: words and tune traditional (as printed by R. R. Terry, 1921), arrangement Stackapella, on R. R. Terry's harmony; licence.arrangement: the soprano Terry's voice part note for note, the other parts the chorus lines only, on his chords, a whole tone higher; licence.source: Terry (1921), No. 15, "Blow my bully boys", pp. 32 and 33, in F major, written here a whole tone higher in G, verses 1, 2, 5, 6, 7 and 8 of Terry's eight; description: a Yankee ship and what her crew got to eat; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'blow-boys-blow' => 'Blow, Boys, Blow is a halliard shanty, sung for hoisting the sails, about a Yankee ship and what her crew got to eat. Richard Terry notes that spotless decks, and masts and yards that "shone like silver", were the marks of a Yankee packet ship, kept that way by a terrible discipline. Our arrangement is read from his Shanty Book of 1921, where it is called Blow my bully boys, a whole tone higher, in G, on the harmony of his piano part. This is six of his eight verses for 2 to 4 singers: the soprano is the shantyman, singing each solo line alone, and everyone answers "Blow, boys blow." and "Blow my bully boys blow."',
    // Sources: song facts [The Jewish Encyclopedia, vol. 8 (1904), "Ma'oz Zur": the first letters of its stanzas spell the poet's name, Mordecai, who probably lived in the thirteenth century; first sung at home after lighting the Hanukkah lights, later in the synagogue too]; song facts [the same article: the Königsberg cantor Eduard Birnbaum traced the tune to an old German folk song, "So weiss ich eins, dass mich erfreut", which Martin Luther also took for one of his first chorales]; [Encyclopaedia Judaica, "Ma'oz Tzur", music section: the likeness rests on isolated motives]; credits: words a Hebrew piyyut signed Mordecai in its acrostic (13th century), tune traditional Ashkenazi, arrangement Stackapella on A. Z. Idelsohn's harmony (1911/12); licence.arrangement: on the harmony of Idelsohn's two and three printed voices; licence.source: Idelsohn, Sefer ha-Shirim (Berlin, 5672, that is 1911/12), No. 83, p. 77, in E major, kept in E major; verses 1 and 5, each with the printed repeat of its last line; transliterated in modern Israeli pronunciation; description: verse 1 praises Hashem, the rock of my salvation, verse 5 tells the Hanukkah story of the oil; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'maoz-tzur' => 'Maoz Tzur is the Hanukkah hymn sung after lighting the Hanukkah lights, at first only at home and later in the synagogue too. The first letters of its first five stanzas spell the poet\'s name, Mordecai, and the cantor Eduard Birnbaum traced its tune to an old German folk song, "So weiss ich eins, dass mich erfreut", though later scholars find only scattered likenesses. Our arrangement is read from A. Z. Idelsohn\'s Sefer ha-Shirim of 1911/12, in E major, on the harmony of his two and three voices. It is sung in Hebrew, transliterated, for 2 to 4 singers: verse 1, praising Hashem, the Rock of my salvation, and verse 5, the Hanukkah story of the oil.',
    // Sources: song facts [Kisselgof, Lider-zamelbukh far der yidisher shul un familye, No. 24, p. 32, read from the scan: his Yiddish songbook "for the Jewish school and family", first published in St Petersburg in 1911 or 1912, the tune marked "Chsidisch", a Hasidic melody]; song facts [the same book, words p. 13: Mordkhe Rivesman's words paint the holiday at home: dreidels every night, sizzling-hot latkes, lighting the thin little candles and saying "Al hanisim", for the miracles]; credits: words Mordkhe Rivesman, tune traditional Hasidic, arrangement Stackapella on Pesach Lvov's arrangement (1911/12), as printed in the fourth edition, which follows the second (1914), revised in part by A. Zhitomirsky (died 1937); Lvov died 22 February 1913 [the book's own memorial page, "tsum ondenk fun P. Lvov", 4th ed. p. 6, signed Z. Kisselgof, January 1914; the Jewish Encyclopedia of Russia (1995): Lvov, Pavel (Pesakh) Ruvimovich, 1880 Kiev to 1913 Nervi]; licence.arrangement: the soprano and alto Lvov's two voices as printed, the tenor and bass from his piano's left hand; licence.source: read from the fourth edition (Berlin), in F minor, kept in F minor; the Yiddish transliterated in YIVO-style romanization; description: verse 2 praises Judah the Maccabee, the bass on "dm"; sections: Verse 1, Verse 2, Verse 1 again; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'oy-chanukah' => 'Oy Chanukah is a Yiddish Hanukkah song that Susman Kisselgof printed in his songbook "for the Jewish school and family", first published in St Petersburg in 1911 or 1912, marking its tune "Chsidisch": a Hasidic melody. Mordkhe Rivesman\'s words paint the holiday at home: dreidels every night, sizzling-hot latkes, lighting the thin little candles and saying "Al hanisim", for the miracles. Our arrangement is read from the book\'s fourth edition, in F minor as printed: the soprano and alto sing Pesach Lvov\'s two voices, and the tenor and bass take his piano\'s left hand. It is sung in Yiddish, transliterated, for 2 to 4 singers: verse 1, verse 2, praising Judah the Maccabee, then verse 1 again.',
    // Sources: song facts [M. M. Warshawsky, Yidishe folkslider (Warsaw: Aleksander Gins, 1901), title page and p. 13, read from the scan: his own songbook, with a foreword by Sholem Aleichem, has it as "Der alef-beys"; the year, Warsaw and the foreword also in the Leksikon of Yiddish writers and the Posen Library]; song facts [Z. Kisselgof, Lider-zamelbukh far der yidisher shul un familye, 4th edition (Berlin: Juval), music part p. 19, No. 13, read from the scan: "for the Jewish school and family", among its secular folk songs, for three voices and piano, "Arrangirt fun P. Lwow"]; credits: words and tune Mark Warshawsky, arrangement Stackapella on Pesach Lvov's arrangement (1911/12); licence.arrangement: the soprano the tune as printed, the alto and tenor from his two alto voices, the bass from his piano's left hand; licence.source: printed in G minor, written here in F minor, verse 1 and the refrain as underlaid, the fourth verse from the words section, romanized in YIVO style; description: soft and moderately quick, in 3/4; sections: Verse 1, Refrain, Verse 2, Refrain again; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'oyfn-pripetchik' => 'Oyfn Pripetchik, "On the hearth", is Mark Warshawsky\'s Yiddish song of a little fire on the hearth, where the rabbi teaches small children their alphabet. His own songbook, printed in Warsaw in 1901 with a foreword by Sholem Aleichem, has it as "Der alef-beys", and Susman Kisselgof\'s songbook "for the Jewish school and family" printed it among its secular folk songs, for three voices and piano, arranged by Pesach Lvov. Our arrangement is read from that book\'s fourth edition, a tone lower in F minor: the soprano sings the tune as printed, the alto and tenor take Lvov\'s two alto voices, and the bass his piano\'s left hand. It is sung in Yiddish, transliterated, for 2 to 4 singers: verse 1 and the book\'s fourth verse, about the tears the children will one day find in the letters, each followed by the refrain "Zogt zhe, kinderlekh", say it, children.',
    // Sources: song facts [Simon Adler, Sulamitha, Op. 46 (New York: Theodor Lohr, 1890), cover and p. 2, read from the scan (Brown Digital Repository bdr:100759): "Arranged by Simon Adler", "Copyright 1890"; Goldfaden died 1908 (Library of Congress authority)]; song facts ["Raisins and Almonds", arr. A. R. Zagler (New York: S. Schenker, about 1910 to 1917), cover, read from the scan (bdr:100085): the Yiddish and English titles, "From The Opera 'Shulamith'", the portrait captioned "A. Goldfaden"]; credits: words and tune Abraham Goldfaden, arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: our own parts under the tune as Zagler's edition prints it, his piano used only to check the chords, the lower voices humming the verse and the refrain's quick bar; licence.source: in E minor in 6/8 as printed, romanized in YIVO style; description: the widowed Daughter of Zion rocks her only son, the little white goat under his cradle, raisins and almonds his trade; sections: Verse, Refrain; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'rozhinkes-mit-mandlen' => 'Rozhinkes mit Mandlen, "Raisins and almonds", is the lullaby from Abraham Goldfaden\'s Yiddish opera Shulamith. In a corner of the Temple the widowed Daughter of Zion rocks her only son to sleep, singing that a little white goat stands under his cradle and that raisins and almonds will be his trade. Its melody was already in a New York piano arrangement from Shulamith, Simon Adler\'s "Raisines & Almonds", printed "Copyright 1890", eighteen years before Goldfaden died, and New York sheet music sold it in Yiddish and English, with Goldfaden\'s portrait on the cover. Our tune and words are read from that sheet music, S. Schenker\'s of about 1910 to 1917, in E minor as printed, and the four parts are our own. It is sung in Yiddish, transliterated, for 2 to 4 singers: the lower voices hum softly under the verse and join in the words of the refrain.',
    // Sources: song facts [The Union Hymnal (CCAR, 1897), No. 17, pp. 140 and 141, read from the scan: the hymnal of the Central Conference of American Rabbis prints it in four parts among its "Hebrew Hymns and Responses", with all five verses]; song facts [The Union Hymnal (1932), No. 292, and (1897), index p. 218: the 1932 edition credits it to Julius Freudenthal; the 1897 edition\'s index gives the music\'s source only as "German"]; credits: words traditional Hebrew liturgy, tune Julius Freudenthal (1843), arrangement the Union Hymnal's four parts (1897), adapted by Stackapella; licence.arrangement: adapted for 2 to 4 beginners; licence.source: the Union Hymnal (1897), in F, written here a tone up in G; verses 1 to 5 sung in order; the words respelled in modern Israeli pronunciation; description: a Hebrew hymn of the Sabbath morning service; its five short verses praise Hashem as our Lord, our King and our Saviour; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'ein-keloheinu' => 'Ein Keloheinu is a Hebrew hymn of the Sabbath morning service. The Union Hymnal of 1897 prints it in four parts among its "Hebrew Hymns and Responses", its index giving the music\'s source only as "German"; the 1932 edition credits it to Julius Freudenthal. Our arrangement is read from the 1897 book, a tone higher than its F, in G. All five short verses are sung in Hebrew, transliterated, for 2 to 4 singers: there is none like Hashem, our Lord, our King, our Saviour; who is like Hashem; we give thanks; blessed is Hashem; You are Hashem. It is sung with the substituted names customary when singing outside prayer: Elokeinu and Kelokeinu.',
    // Sources: song facts [The Jewish Encyclopedia, vol. 1 (1901), "Addir Hu", and A. Z. Idelsohn, Jewish Music in its Historical Development (1929), p. 174: the earliest known printing of its tune, in an early form, in a Haggadah in Hebrew, Latin and German that J. S. Rittangel, a professor at Königsberg, published in 1644]; credits: words traditional Hebrew hymn of the Passover Haggadah, tune traditional, arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: original, written for Stackapella; licence.source: The Union Haggadah, revised edition (CCAR, 1923), p. 81, in C, voice and piano, written here in C as printed, the piano part used only to check the chords; three verses; the words respelled in modern Israeli pronunciation; description: Adir Hu ("Mighty is He"), the Passover Seder song; the three verses the Union Haggadah prints, of the hymn's eight; each asks Hashem to build His house soon, speedily, in our days; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'adir-hu' => 'Adir Hu, "Mighty is He", is a song of the Passover Seder. The earliest known printing of its tune, in an early form, is in a Haggadah in Hebrew, Latin and German that J. S. Rittangel published in 1644. Our tune and words are read from the Union Haggadah of 1923, in C as printed, and the four parts are our own. It is sung in Hebrew, transliterated, for 2 to 4 singers: the book\'s three short verses, which take their names for Hashem from across the hymn\'s eight stanzas, each praising Hashem and asking Him to build His house soon, in our days. It is sung with the substituted name customary when singing outside prayer: Kel.',
    // Sources: song facts [A. Z. Idelsohn, Jewish Music in its Historical Development (copyright 1929; read from the scan of the 1944 Tudor printing), p. 401, Table XXXII, No. 1: printed in his history of Jewish music among the "Tunes of the Eastern Ashkenazic folk-song"]; Idelsohn (1929), pp. 360 to 361, the archive.org text of the 1944 printing: songs of Elijah sung at the outgoing of Sabbath already in the eleventh century, this refrain the oldest part; song facts [the same page, the words under the tune: they call on the prophet Elijah by three names, Elijah the prophet, Elijah the Tishbite, Elijah of Gilead]; credits: words traditional Hebrew refrain, tune traditional Eastern Ashkenazi folk tune (as printed by A. Z. Idelsohn, 1929), arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: original, written for Stackapella; licence.source: Idelsohn (1929), p. 401, in E minor, the tune alone, no harmony; his text on p. 392 calls it one of two very popular tunes for 'Eliyohu Hannovi'; written here in G minor, a minor third up; only the metrical first section, up to the print's Fine, sung three times; respelled in modern Israeli pronunciation; description: the old Hebrew refrain that, as Idelsohn wrote, Jews have sung at the close of the Sabbath since the Middle Ages; sung three times through, the third time softly; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'eliyahu-hanavi' => 'Eliyahu HaNavi, "Elijah the Prophet", is an old Hebrew refrain sung at the close of the Sabbath, which A. Z. Idelsohn called the oldest part of the Elijah songs that Jewish communities already sang there in the eleventh century. It calls on the prophet by three names: the prophet, the Tishbite, the Gileadite. Our tune and words come from a 1944 printing of Idelsohn\'s 1929 history of Jewish music, among its "Tunes of the Eastern Ashkenazic folk-song". It is a minor third above his E minor, in G minor, with four parts of our own. Sung in Hebrew, transliterated, for 2 to 4 singers: the refrain\'s first part, those three names, three times through, the third time softly.',
    // Sources: song facts [The Union Haggadah (CCAR, 1923), pp. 86 to 92, read from the scan: it counts from one to thirteen, and each stanza repeats every number back down to one: one is Hashem, in heaven and on earth]; song facts [The Union Haggadah (1923), pp. 87 to 89: it changes two of the numbers, in its Hebrew as well as its English: eight becomes the eight days of Hanukkah, and nine the nine festivals]; credits: words traditional Hebrew counting song of the Passover Haggadah, tune traditional, arrangement Stackapella; licence.words: part of the Haggadah since about the fifteenth century, as the Union Haggadah's notes say; licence.tune: printed without a composer in the Union Haggadah (1923); licence.arrangement: original, written for Stackapella; licence.source: The Union Haggadah, revised edition (1923), p. 93, in D minor, voice and piano, written here in F minor, a minor third up, the piano part used only to check the chords; stanzas 1 to 4 sung; respelled in modern Israeli pronunciation; description: the counting song sung near the end of the Passover Seder; four mothers, three fathers, the two tablets of the covenant, and the one, Hashem, in heaven and on earth; sung here from one to four; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'echad-mi-yodea' => 'Echad Mi Yodea, "Who Knows One?", is the counting song sung at the Passover Seder, in Ashkenazi Haggadot since at least the sixteenth century; its earliest known printing is Prague, 1590. It counts from one to thirteen, each stanza counting back down to one. Our tune and words are read from the Union Haggadah of 1923, which prints the tune in D minor; here it is a minor third higher, in F minor, with four parts of our own. It is sung in Hebrew, transliterated, for 2 to 4 singers: verses one to four, from one is Hashem, in heaven and on earth, to the four mothers. It is sung with the substituted name customary when singing outside prayer: Elokeinu.',
    // Sources: song facts [The Union Haggadah (CCAR, 1923), pp. 84 and 85, read from the scan: printed as a solo verse and a chorus; one singer leads each verse, and everyone answers "Lecha ulecha", yours and yours]; song facts [The Union Haggadah (CCAR, 1908), pp. 54 and 55, read from the scan: already in the book's first edition of 1908, the same tune in the same key, with only its first verse]; credits: words traditional Hebrew hymn of the Passover Haggadah, tune traditional, arrangement Stackapella; licence.words: already in the seder of Jacob ben Judah of London's Etz Hayyim, dated 1287, so older than the fifteenth century the Union Haggadah's notes give; licence.tune: in the Union Haggadah (1908), printed as 'Traditional' in its revised edition (1923); [David Kaufmann, "The Ritual of the Seder and the Agada of the English Jews before the Expulsion", JQR 4 (July 1892), pp. 550 and 560 to 561; Encyclopaedia Judaica (Roth): Etz Hayyim "c. 1286"]; licence.arrangement: original, written for Stackapella; licence.source: The Union Haggadah, revised edition (1923), pp. 84 to 85, in C, voice and piano, written here in D, a tone up, the piano part used only to check the chords; the three verses it prints sung here; the soprano sings the solo verses alone and everyone sings the chorus, as printed; respelled in modern Israeli pronunciation; description: Ki Lo Na'eh ("For to Him it is fitting"), the Passover Seder hymn; each verse praises Hashem as mighty, holy and strong in His kingship; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'ki-lo-naeh' => 'Ki Lo Na\'eh, "For to Him it is fitting", is a Passover Seder hymn. It was already sung in England before 1290: Jacob ben Judah of London wrote it into his book of Jewish law, dated 1287. The Union Haggadah prints it as a solo verse and chorus: one singer leads each verse, and everyone answers "Lecha ulecha", yours and yours. Our tune and words are read from the 1923 edition, a tone up, in D, with four parts of our own. It is sung in Hebrew, transliterated, for 2 to 4 singers: three verses praising Hashem as mighty, holy and strong in His kingship. It is sung with the substituted name customary when singing outside prayer: Adoshem.',
    // Sources: song facts [The Jewish Encyclopedia, vol. 7 (1904), p. 675, "Lekah Dodi": Solomon ha-Levi Alkabetz wrote it about the middle of the sixteenth century to welcome the Sabbath, and signed eight of its nine verses with his name, their first letters spelling it out]; song facts [the same article: the Jewish Encyclopedia says this tune was kept for the weeks of the Omer, between Passover and Shavuot]; credits: words Solomon ha-Levi Alkabetz (16th century), tune traditional Ashkenazi melody for the Omer weeks (printed by Abraham Baer, 1877), arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: original, written for Stackapella (the print is a melody only); licence.source: Abraham Baer, Baal T'fillah, 3rd ed. (Frankfurt, after 1883; the first edition, Gothenburg 1877, has the same numbers and notes), Nos. 340 and 333, for the Sabbaths of the Sefirah, G major, every pitch as printed, checked against The Jewish Encyclopedia, vol. 7 (1904), p. 677; written here in G as printed; the Hebrew fitted by us in modern Israeli pronunciation, the refrain sung first, then after each verse; verses 1 and 9; "Kel" and "Adoshem"; description: the Friday-night hymn that greets the Sabbath as a bride, to the old Ashkenazi melody kept for the weeks of the Omer; song file (the parts' guide notes and lyrics)
    'lecha-dodi' => 'Lecha Dodi, "Come, my beloved", is the Friday-night hymn that greets the Sabbath as a bride. Solomon ha-Levi Alkabetz wrote it in the sixteenth century, and the first letters of eight of its nine verses spell his name. This tune, which the Jewish Encyclopedia of 1904 says was kept for the weeks of the Omer, is read from the cantor Abraham Baer\'s Baal T\'fillah, whose first edition appeared in Gothenburg in 1877 (we read its third edition, with the same notes), in G as he printed it; the Hebrew is fitted by us, and the four parts are our own. It is sung in Hebrew, transliterated, for 2 to 4 singers: the refrain, then verses 1 and 9, each followed by it. It is sung with the substituted names customary when singing outside prayer: Kel and Adoshem.',
    // Sources: song facts [The Jewish Encyclopedia, vol. 12 (1906), p. 656, "Zemirot": one of the zemirot, the Hebrew songs sung at home around the table, most of all before or after the grace after the Sabbath's main meals]; song facts [the same article, p. 659: the Jewish Encyclopedia names this German melody as the most frequent air for the table songs, sung with this song or with another, "Yom Zeh le-Yisrael"]; credits: words traditional Hebrew Sabbath table hymn (zemer), anonymous, tune German melody for the Sabbath zemirot (printed by Francis L. Cohen, 1906), arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: original, written for Stackapella (the print is a melody only); licence.source: The Jewish Encyclopedia, vol. 12 (1906), p. 658, "SABBATH REST", G minor, printed with three English verses; written here in G minor as printed; the Hebrew fitted by us in modern Israeli pronunciation, verse 1 and verse 4, the refrain sung first, between the verses and last; "Adoshem" (and "kadoshem") for the four-letter name; description: a Sabbath table song; the refrain calls the faithful to bless the Rock whose food we have eaten; verse 1 thanks the One who feeds the world, verse 4 asks for the Temple to be rebuilt; song file (the parts' guide notes and lyrics)
    'tzur-mishelo' => 'Tzur Mishelo is one of the zemirot, the Hebrew songs sung at home at table, most of all before or after the grace after the Sabbath\'s main meals. The Jewish Encyclopedia names this German melody as the most frequent air for the table songs, sung with this song or with another, "Yom Zeh le-Yisrael". Our tune is read from its 1906 printing, in G minor as printed; the Hebrew is fitted by us, and the four parts are our own. It is sung in Hebrew, transliterated, for 2 to 4 singers: the refrain, blessing the Rock whose food we have eaten, around verses 1 and 4. It is sung with the substituted name customary when singing outside prayer: Adoshem.',
    // Sources: song facts [The Jewish Encyclopedia, vol. 1 (1901), p. 205, "Adon 'Olam": in Sephardic and British synagogues the whole congregation sings it at the close of the Sabbath and festival morning services, and it is chanted almost everywhere on the eve of Yom Kippur]; song facts [the same article, p. 206, citing Baer's prayer-book commentary (1868): its closing lines, "Into His hand I commit my spirit when I fall asleep and when I awake", led Seligman Baer to think it was first meant to be said before going to sleep]; credits: words Hebrew hymn, author unknown (medieval), tune old synagogue tune ("A. W.", alte Weise), as Eliezer Gerovitsch printed it (1897), arrangement Stackapella, on Eliezer Gerovitsch's harmony (1897); licence.arrangement: original, written for Stackapella on the four voices Gerovitsch printed for the opening strain, adapted for 2 to 4 beginners; licence.source: Gerovitsch, Shirei Tefilah, part II, 2nd edition (Rostov-on-Don, 1897), No. 16, "Adon olom. A. W.", four voices, D minor; only the opening strain marked "A. W." (the old tune he harmonised) used; written here in D minor as printed; sung four times, couplets 1 to 6, 9 and 10; the four-letter name sung as "Adoshem"; description: the hymn that closes Sabbath and festival services, to an old synagogue tune that Eliezer Gerovitsch harmonised in 1897; song file (the parts' guide notes and lyrics); the 1897 edition read from the UB Frankfurt scan (Freimann collection, urn:nbn:de:hebis:30:1-131852, image 1355382); story adon-olam, checked 2026-11-13
    'adon-olam' => 'Adon Olam, "Lord of the world", is a hymn that many congregations sing together at the close of the Sabbath and festival morning services. Its last lines, "into His hand I commit my spirit when I sleep and when I wake", led Seligman Baer to think it was meant for bedtime. Our tune is read from Eliezer Gerovitsch\'s Shirei Tefilah, in an edition of 1897, which marks it as old, in D minor as printed, with our four parts built on his harmony. It is sung in Hebrew, transliterated, for 2 to 4 singers: his strain four times, carrying lines 1 to 6, 9 and 10 of the poem. It is sung with the substituted name customary when singing outside prayer: Adoshem.',
    // Sources: song facts [Abraham Baer, Baal T'fillah (3rd edition), p. 59, Nos. 196 and 197: it is sung on Purim once the Book of Esther has been read: Baer's book has the scroll rolled up, the blessing sung after it, and then this song]; credits: words traditional Hebrew Purim hymn, tune synagogue tune, as Abraham Baer printed it (after 1883), arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: original, written for Stackapella (the print is a solo melody); licence.source: Baer, Baal T'fillah oder Der practische Vorbeter, 3rd edition (Frankfurt am Main, after 1883), No. 197, "Schoschannas Jakow", p. 59, B-flat major, with its quiet last line ("Leise weiter"); written here in B-flat as printed; the print's words respelled in modern Israeli pronunciation; sung twice, then the last line once; description: the Purim song sung after the reading of the Megillah, to the cantor's tune Abraham Baer printed; Jacob's rose rejoiced when they saw Mordechai in royal blue; none who trust in Him are put to shame; sung twice, then softly its last line, Charvona too is remembered for good; song file (the parts' guide notes and lyrics)
    'shoshanat-yaakov' => 'Shoshanat Yaakov, "The rose of Jacob", is the Purim song sung once the Book of Esther has been read: Abraham Baer\'s Baal T\'fillah has the scroll rolled up, the blessing sung after it, and then this song. Jacob\'s rose rejoiced to see Mordechai in royal blue, and none who trust in Him are put to shame. Our tune and words are read from the third edition of Baer\'s book, printed after 1883 (the tune is already in its first edition, of 1877), in B-flat as printed, and the four parts are our own. It is sung in Hebrew, transliterated, for 2 to 4 singers: the whole song twice, then softly its last line, Charvona too is remembered for good.',
    // Sources: song facts [John Julian, A Dictionary of Hymnology (1907), vol. 1, p. 683, "Lobe den Herren", citing Lauxmann, read from the scan: the favourite hymn of King Friedrich Wilhelm III of Prussia, who was affected by hearing it sung in a boat in the mines at Waldenburg in 1800]; story notes (seen, the same page): based on Psalm 103:1-6 and Psalm 150; earliest known printing in Neander's Glaub- und Liebesübung (Bremen, 1680); the tune from the Stralsund Gesangbuch of 1665, adapted by Neander; credits: words Joachim Neander (1680), translated by Catherine Winkworth (1863), tune German chorale "Lobe den Herren" (earliest known printing: Stralsund Gesangbuch, 1665), arrangement William Sterndale Bennett and Otto Goldschmidt (1863), adapted by Stackapella; licence.arrangement: the four parts of The Chorale Book for England, the tune as printed and the bass mostly as printed, adapted for beginners; licence.source: The Chorale Book for England (Longman, 1863), No. 9, tune lxii, in G as printed, verses 1 and 2; description: the first two verses, broad and joyful; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'praise-to-the-lord' => 'Praise to the Lord, the Almighty is Catherine Winkworth\'s English of Lobe den Herren, Joachim Neander\'s German hymn of praise, printed in 1680 and based on Psalms 103 and 150. Its tune is older: its earliest known printing is in a Stralsund hymnbook of 1665, set to other words, and John Julian\'s Dictionary of Hymnology says Neander adapted it. Julian also calls it the favourite hymn of King Friedrich Wilhelm III of Prussia, and passes on Lauxmann\'s account of how the king was moved to hear it sung in a boat in the mines at Waldenburg in 1800. Our arrangement is read from The Chorale Book for England of 1863, which prints Winkworth\'s words with four parts edited by William Sterndale Bennett and Otto Goldschmidt, in G, adapted for beginners. These are the first two verses, for 2 to 4 singers, broad and joyful.',
    // Sources: song facts [Henry Alford, The Poetical Works of Henry Alford (London, 1865), p. 300, footnote, read from the scan: "much disfigured by alterations made without the author's consent"; "responsible for this form of it only"]; story notes (seen): the same book dates the hymn "After Harvest. (1844.)"; Hymns Ancient and Modern (Novello, 1861), Hymn 223, the earliest printing of these words with this tune our reader found, its Index of Tunes naming Elvey as composer and harmonizer of "St. George"; verse 4 as Alford revised it in The Year of Praise (1867), Hymn 275; gloss: Harvest-home, the time of harvest and its feast (Webster, An American Dictionary of the English Language, 1828: "The time of harvest", and "the feast made at the gathering of corn"); credits: words Henry Alford (1844; verse 4 as he revised it for The Year of Praise, 1867), tune George J. Elvey, "St. George" (1858; so named in Hymns Ancient and Modern, 1861), arrangement Elvey's four parts (Hymns Ancient and Modern, 1861), adapted by Stackapella; licence.source: Hymns Ancient and Modern (1861), Hymn 223, in G as printed; verses 1 and 4; description: the first verse and the last, steady and bright, every line one breath; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'come-ye-thankful-people' => 'Come, Ye Thankful People, Come is Henry Alford\'s harvest hymn, dated 1844 in his collected poems. Harvest-home was the old name for the time of harvest and its feast. In 1865 Alford complained that his hymn had been "much disfigured by alterations made without the author\'s consent", and said he was "responsible for this form of it only". Its tune, St. George, is George J. Elvey\'s, and Hymns Ancient and Modern set the hymn to it in 1861, with Elvey\'s own four parts. Our arrangement is read from that page, in G, adapted for beginners. These are the first verse and the last, for 2 to 4 singers, steady and bright, every line one breath; the last is Alford\'s own revised verse of 1867, "Even so, Lord, quickly come".',
    // Sources: song facts [Lyra Germanica, second series: The Christian Life, tr. Catherine Winkworth (Longman, 1858), p. 145, read from the scan: titled "The Chorus of God's Thankful Children"]; song facts [The English Hymnal (1906 edition), preface and Hymn 533, read from the scan: "It is distressing to hear 'Nun Danket' or 'St. Anne' raced through at about twice the proper speed."; marked "Very slow and majestic"]; credits: words Martin Rinkart, "Nun danket alle Gott", translated by Catherine Winkworth (Lyra Germanica, second series, 1858), tune Johann Crüger, "Nun danket", arrangement Felix Mendelssohn's harmony as The Hymnal (1916) prints it, adapted by Stackapella; licence.tune: The Hymnal of 1916 dates the tune 1647; Zahn and Julian date it 1648; licence.source: The Hymnal (Church Pension Fund, 1916), Hymn 422, "Deo Gratias", headed "Johann Crüger, 1647. Harmonized by Felix Mendelssohn, 1809-1847.", in F as printed, verses 1 and 2; description: slow and broad; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'now-thank-we-all-our-god' => 'Now Thank We All Our God is Catherine Winkworth\'s English of Martin Rinkart\'s German hymn Nun danket alle Gott. She printed it in 1858, in her Lyra Germanica, under the title "The Chorus of God\'s Thankful Children". The tune is Johann Crüger\'s, and the earliest known printing of words and tune together is in his hymn book Praxis pietatis melica, Berlin, of 1647 or 1648. It is meant to be sung broadly: the preface of The English Hymnal of 1906 complained, "It is distressing to hear \'Nun Danket\' or \'St. Anne\' raced through at about twice the proper speed", and the book marked the tune "Very slow and majestic". Our arrangement is read from The Hymnal (1916), on the harmony it credits to Felix Mendelssohn, in F as printed, adapted for beginners. These are verses 1 and 2, for 2 to 4 singers, slow and broad.',
    // Sources: song facts [Adrianus Valerius, Nederlandtsche Gedenck-clanck (Haarlem, 1626), p. 170, read from the scan: the Dutch words, "Wilt heden nu treden voor God den Heere", printed to an older tune, "Hey wilder dan wild", with lute tablature underneath]; song facts [The Abingdon Hymnal, ed. Earl Enyeart Harper (Abingdon Press, 1928), No. 207, footnote, read from the scan: the first two verses in unison, men and women taking turns if they like, and the last "in full harmony"]; credits: words Theodore Baker (1894), from the Dutch "Wilt heden nu treden" in Valerius (1626), tune "Kremser", a Netherlands folk tune printed by Valerius (1626), as Eduard Kremser arranged it, arrangement the four parts for Kremser as The Beacon Hymnal printed them (1924), adapted by Stackapella; licence.words: Baker's English published 1894 ("English version copyright, 1894, by G. Schirmer", The American Student Hymnal, 1928); licence.arrangement: the same parts printed as "Arr. by E. Kremser" in The Abingdon Hymnal (1928); licence.source: The Beacon Hymnal, ed. Florence Buck (Beacon Press, 1924), No. 17, in D as printed, verses 1 and 2; description: two verses, steady and strong in three; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'we-gather-together' => 'We Gather Together is Theodore Baker\'s English, published in 1894, of an old Dutch song of thanks, made from Eduard Kremser\'s German setting of 1877. The Dutch words, "Wilt heden nu treden", have their earliest known printing in Adrianus Valerius\'s Nederlandtsche Gedenck-clanck, which his heirs had printed at Haarlem in 1626, after his death, set to an older tune, "Hey wilder dan wild" ("Hey, wilder than wild"), with lute tablature underneath. The tune is called Kremser, after him. A 1928 American hymnal told congregations to sing the first two verses in unison, men and women taking turns if they liked, and only the last "in full harmony". Our arrangement is read from the four parts The Beacon Hymnal printed in 1924, which The Abingdon Hymnal credits to Kremser, in D as printed, adapted for beginners. These are verses 1 and 2, for 2 to 4 singers, in harmony, steady and strong in three.',
    // Sources: song facts [The Scots Musical Museum, vol. 5 (Johnson, Edinburgh; dated 1796 by the National Library of Scotland), pp. 430 and 431, Nos. 417 and 418, read from the scan: Burns's own words, "O Jenny's a' weet, poor body", to one tune, then a "2d Sett" with the tune sung today, to older words]; song facts [G. F. Graham, The Songs of Scotland, vol. II (1849), p. 11, quoting Stenhouse, read from the scan: the music-seller John Watlen "altered the first strain of the former tune a little, and published it with the new words. His edition had a considerable run."]; credits: words traditional Scottish, after Robert Burns, tune traditional Scottish, arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: original, written for Stackapella; licence.source: Graham, The Songs of Scotland, vol. II (Wood and Co., Edinburgh, 1849), pp. 10 to 11, arranged by T. M. Mudie, in G as printed; verse 1 under the notes and Graham's next stanza ("Comin' frae the well"); Mudie's piano part used only to check the chords; licence.source: Graham footnotes "Gin" as "If" and "Ilka" as "Each; every"; description: two verses of "Gin a body meet a body"; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'comin-thro-the-rye' => 'Comin\' Thro\' the Rye is a Scots song that Robert Burns reworked from an older one for James Johnson\'s Scots Musical Museum. The earliest printing we have seen is in the Museum\'s fifth volume, which libraries date to 1796. It gives the song twice: Burns\'s set, with the chorus "Oh Jenny\'s a\' weet poor body", to one tune, then on the next page an unsigned "2d Sett", the tune sung today, with an earlier form of our words. William Stenhouse later wrote that the music-seller John Watlen "altered the first strain of the former tune a little, and published it with the new words. His edition had a considerable run." John Glen dated Watlen\'s printing of that second set to 1794, before the Museum\'s. Our tune and words are read from G. F. Graham\'s Songs of Scotland of 1848, in G, with four parts of our own. "Gin" means "if", and "ilka" "each". This is two verses for 2 to 4 singers, at a moderate pace.',
    // Sources: song facts [G. F. Graham, The Songs of Scotland, vol. II (Wood and Co., Edinburgh), p. 29, read from the scan: the song "now invariably sung to a modern version of 'Low down in the broom'"; story notes (seen): vol. II dated 1849]; song facts [The Scots Musical Museum, vol. 5 (Johnson, Edinburgh; dated 1796 by the National Library of Scotland), p. 415, Nos. 402 and 403, read from the scan: Burns's words set to "Major Graham" and to an "Old Set"; the Museum's two settings (1796)]; story [^urbani]: the song's earliest known printing, Urbani's Selection of Scots Songs, Book 2 (entered at Stationers' Hall 1794), p. 16, read from the scan: "The red red Rose", "The Music by P. Urbani"; credits: words Robert Burns, tune traditional Scottish, "Low down in the Broom", as printed by R. A. Smith, arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: original, written for Stackapella over R. A. Smith's piano chords; licence.source: Smith, The Scotish Minstrel, vol. 3 (Robert Purdie, Edinburgh; undated, early 1820s), p. 85, in C as printed, its index giving the words as Burns's to the air "Low down in the Broom"; the page's two verses; description: two verses, slow and tender; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'red-red-rose' => 'My Love Is Like a Red, Red Rose is Robert Burns\'s love song. Its earliest known printing, in Peter Urbani\'s Selection of Scots Songs (1794), has Urbani\'s own tune. The fifth volume of James Johnson\'s Scots Musical Museum, which libraries date to 1796, sets it to a strathspey called Major Graham, with an "Old Set" too. By the late 1840s, G. F. Graham could write that it was "now invariably sung to a modern version of \'Low down in the broom\'", the tune we sing. Our arrangement is read from R. A. Smith\'s Scotish Minstrel of the early 1820s, which prints Burns\'s words to that air, in C, with four parts of our own over Smith\'s piano chords. This is two verses for 2 to 4 singers, slow and tender, each ending on Smith\'s repeated lines, "\'Till a\' the seas gang dry" and "Tho\' \'twere ten thousand mile".',
    // Sources: song facts [Alfred Moffat, The Minstrelsy of Scotland (Augener, 2nd edition), p. 109, footnote, read from the scan: Lady John Scott told him the tune was her own, and that she altered the second verse and wrote all of the third]; song facts [C. K. Sharpe, A Ballad Book (1880 reprint of the 1823 edition), pp. 107 and 108, note, read from the scan: the older song "said to" be by Mr Douglas of Fingland, for Anne, a daughter of Sir Robert Laurie of Maxwelton; "the Lady afterwards married Mr Ferguson of Craigdarroch"]; licence.tune: the earliest copy in the British Museum is marked 1847 (Moffat, p. 109, footnote); credits: words an older Scottish song, said to be by Douglas of Fingland, reworked by Lady John Scott, tune Lady John Scott, arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: original, written for Stackapella, its chords checked against the Hall printing's unsigned piano part and Finlay Dun's arrangement; licence.source: Annie Laurie (New York: William Hall & Son, plate 793; dated about 1848 by the Washington University catalogue), pp. 2 to 4, no composer, poet or arranger named, the cover a portrait of Jenny Lind, in C as printed; verses 1 and 2; story notes (seen): its words "Gie'd", "fa's", "dee"; description: two verses, slow and tender; the alto joins the tune on the low notes and steps above it at the cadences; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'annie-laurie' => 'Annie Laurie grew from an older Scottish song. C. K. Sharpe\'s Ballad Book says that song is "said to" be by Mr Douglas of Fingland, for Anne, a daughter of Sir Robert Laurie of Maxwelton, though "the Lady afterwards married Mr Ferguson of Craigdarroch". Lady John Scott told the editor Alfred Moffat that the tune was her own, and that she altered the second verse and wrote all of the third. The song\'s earliest known printing, without her name, is in The Vocal Melodies of Scotland (Edinburgh, 1838). Our arrangement is read from an early New York printing, with Jenny Lind on its cover, that names no composer or poet, in C, with four parts of our own. "Dee" is Scots for "die". This is the first two verses for 2 to 4 singers, slow and tender.',
    // Sources: song facts [Charles Rogers, Life and Songs of the Baroness Nairne (1869), memoir pp. xxxvi to xxxvii and lxxvii to lxxviii, read from the scan: she kept her authorship secret, writing as "Mrs. Bogan, of Bogan"; only after she died did her sister agree, on January 16, 1846, to "letting it be known that she wrote them"]; song facts [George Henderson, Lady Nairne and Her Songs (1900), pp. 19 to 22, read from the scan: the table at Gask, "Charles, Prince of Wales, breakfasted at this table in the low Drawing Room at Gask, September 11th, 1745."]; credits: words Carolina Oliphant, Lady Nairne (1766 to 1845), tune as printed under Finlay Dun's name (1795 to 1853), arrangement Stackapella; licence.tune: Gems of Scottish Song (Ditson, Boston; the printing catalogued as 1883), p. 107, headed "Finlay Dun."; the page does not say whether he wrote the air or arranged it; licence.arrangement: original, written for Stackapella, its chords checked against Dun's printed piano part; licence.source: the same page, in F as printed; verse 2's words from the 1894 printing, p. 119; verses 1 and 2 of the five printed, each followed by the chorus; description: two verses, each with the chorus, tender and moderate; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'will-ye-no-come-back-again' => 'Will Ye No Come Back Again? is Lady Nairne\'s farewell to Bonnie Prince Charlie, "now awa; Safely owre the friendly main": gone over the sea. Carolina Oliphant, Lady Nairne, kept her authorship secret, even writing as "Mrs. Bogan, of Bogan"; only after she died did her sister agree, in January 1846, to "letting it be known that she wrote them". At Gask, where she grew up, a small table reads "Charles, Prince of Wales, breakfasted at this table in the low Drawing Room at Gask, September 11th, 1745." Our tune is read from Gems of Scottish Song, in the Boston printing catalogued as 1883, in F, where it is headed "Finlay Dun."; the page doesn\'t say whether he wrote it or arranged it. Verse 2\'s words are from the book\'s 1894 printing, and the four parts are our own. This is two verses, each with the chorus, for 2 to 4 singers.',
    // Sources: song facts [The Scots Musical Museum, vol. 4 (Edinburgh: James Johnson, 1792), index p. VI, No. 374, read from the scan: "the Music by Mr James Millar Writer in Edinr."; in Scotland, a writer was a lawyer]; song facts [William Stenhouse, Illustrations of the Lyric Poetry and Music of Scotland (Blackwood, 1853), pp. 346 and 347, quoting Burns's letter to George Thomson of November 1794, read from the scan: Stephen Clarke told Miller, "partly by way of joke", "to keep to the black keys of the harpsichord, and preserve some kind of rhythm, and he would infallibly compose a Scots air"; Clarke fashioned Miller's "rudiments of an air" into the tune]; story notes (seen): earliest known printing in the Museum, vol. 4 (1792), No. 374, "The Banks o' Doon"; credits: words Robert Burns (printed 1792), tune "The Caledonian Hunt's Delight", by James Miller, fashioned by Stephen Clarke (printed 1792; sung as G. F. Graham printed it in 1849), arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: original, written for Stackapella; licence.source: Burns's words as Johnson printed them (1792), both stanzas, to the tune as G. F. Graham printed it in The Songs of Scotland, vol. 1 (Wood & Co., 1849), pp. 4 and 5, in G, Johnson's notes in Graham's smoother rhythm; description: both verses, slow and tender, in a gentle 6/8; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'ye-banks-and-braes' => 'Ye Banks and Braes o\' Bonnie Doon is Robert Burns\'s song of a broken heart by the river Doon. Its earliest known printing is in 1792, in the fourth volume of James Johnson\'s Scots Musical Museum, whose index credits the music to "Mr James Millar Writer in Edinr.": in Scotland, a writer was a lawyer. Burns later told how the organist Stephen Clarke, "partly by way of joke", told Miller "to keep to the black keys of the harpsichord, and preserve some kind of rhythm, and he would infallibly compose a Scots air"; Clarke shaped the result into this tune. We sing Burns\'s words as Johnson printed them, to the tune as G. F. Graham printed it in 1848, in the second volume of his Songs of Scotland, in G, with the alto, tenor and bass our own: both verses, for 2 to 4 singers, slow and tender.',
    // Sources: song facts [an undated Cramer reprint of Songs of the North, vol. I, ed. A. C. Macleod and Harold Boulton, No. V, p. 18, read from the scan: "Four bars taken down from Hebridean boatmen, four bars added by A. C. Macleod."; story notes (seen): its footnote, an episode in Prince Charlie's wanderings in the winter of 1745-6, escaping "with Flora Macdonald and a few devoted Highland boatmen in a rising storm"]; story [Laura Alexandrine Smith, The Music of the Waters (Kegan Paul, 1888), pp. 90 to 91, music and words: "Thunder-clouds rend the air"; the Lyon in Mourning, fol. 529, and Blaikie p. 53: the crossing on 28 June 1746]; song facts [J. S. Blackie, Scottish Song (Blackwood, 1889), p. 239: "Thunder-clouds rend the air"; Our National Songs (Cramer, 1923), No. 10, and the undated reprint of Songs of the North: "Thunderclaps"; read from the scans]; credits: words Harold Boulton, tune a Hebridean boatmen's air, four bars added by Anne Campbell Macleod, arrangement Malcolm Lawson (Songs of the North), adapted by Stackapella; licence.words: printed by 1888 and with its tune in 1889 (Blackie), and the story: with its tune in 1888 (Smith); licence.arrangement: Lawson's chords kept bar by bar; the alto and tenor ours; licence.source: Songs of the North, vol. I, No. V, pp. 18 to 20, in G as printed; chorus, verse 1, chorus, verse 2, chorus, as the book directs ("Chorus to begin, and after each verse"), verses 3 and 4 left out; description: the storm, and Flora's watch over the sleeping prince; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'skye-boat-song' => 'The Skye Boat Song sets Harold Boulton\'s words to an air that, as Songs of the North tells it, is half collected and half composed: "Four bars taken down from Hebridean boatmen, four bars added by A. C. Macleod." The book\'s footnote places it among Prince Charlie\'s wanderings "in the winter of 1745-6", escaping "with Flora Macdonald and a few devoted Highland boatmen in a rising storm"; the crossing was in June 1746. The earliest dated printings with the tune that we have seen, in Laura Alexandrine Smith\'s The Music of the Waters (1888) and J. S. Blackie\'s Scottish Song (1889), sing "Thunder-clouds rend the air"; we sing "Thunderclaps", as our undated copy of Songs of the North and Boulton\'s Our National Songs of 1923 do. Our arrangement is read from Songs of the North, in G, on Malcolm Lawson\'s harmony from the book. This is the chorus and the first two verses, the chorus after each as the book directs, for 2 to 4 singers.',
    // Sources: song facts [Mary Hazzard's manuscript songbook, Winterthur Library, ASC 893, page 108: no title, signed only "North Family", with no village, giver or date]; song facts [the same songbook, title page and pages 105, 108 and 116: "A Collection of Extra Songs of Various Kinds Written and Pricked For the Purpose of Retaining them"; between songs dated 1851 and 1852]; story notes (seen): words as written, "I will bow & be simple I will bow and be free, I will bow and be humble yea bow like the willow tree"; credits: words and tune Shaker song (Mary Hazzard's songbook, about 1851), arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: original, written for Stackapella; licence.source: the manuscript's page 108, in Shaker letteral notation in C, sung in C with every value doubled, every pitch as written; both strains sung twice, as the repeat signs mark them; "Courtesy Winterthur Museum, Garden & Library."; description: the alto and tenor close beneath the tune, the bass holds the roots on "oo"; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'i-will-bow-and-be-simple' => 'I Will Bow and Be Simple is a Shaker song of humility: "I will bow and be humble, yea bow like the willow tree". It survives in Mary Hazzard\'s manuscript songbook, begun in 1847, which calls itself "A Collection of Extra Songs of Various Kinds Written and Pricked For the Purpose of Retaining them". There the song has no title and is signed only "North Family", with no village, giver or date; it comes between songs dated 1851 and 1852. Our tune and words are read from her page, written in the Shakers\' notation of letters, in C, with every pitch as written, and the four parts are our own. This is both strains, each sung twice as the book marks it, for 2 to 4 singers. Courtesy Winterthur Museum, Garden & Library.',
    // Sources: song facts [Mary Hazzard's manuscript songbook, Winterthur Library, ASC 893, page 70: headed "Valuable Riches" and signed "Holy Ground", with no giver or date]; song facts [the same songbook, pages 70 and 72: two pages on comes "Elder Joseph Brackett's Song March 1849"]; story notes (seen): words as written, "O the beautiful treasure laid up for the wise ... Far brighter than diamonds on Princes brow"; songbook begun February 7th 1847; credits: words and tune Shaker song (Mary Hazzard's songbook, about 1849), arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: original, written for Stackapella; licence.source: the manuscript's page 70, in Shaker letteral notation in A minor, sung a fourth lower, in E minor, with every value doubled, every pitch as written; both strains sung twice, as the repeat signs mark them; "Courtesy Winterthur Museum, Garden & Library."; description: the minor-key tune, the alto and tenor close beneath it, the bass holds the roots on "oo"; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'o-the-beautiful-treasure' => 'O the Beautiful Treasure is a Shaker song of the treasure "laid up for the wise", "Far brighter than diamonds on Princes\' brow, And richer than royalty can bestow." It survives in Mary Hazzard\'s manuscript songbook, begun in 1847, where it is headed "Valuable Riches" and signed "Holy Ground", with no giver or date; two pages on comes "Elder Joseph Brackett\'s Song March 1849". Our tune and words are read from her page, written in the Shakers\' notation of letters, a fourth lower, in E minor, with every pitch as written except the octave of the last two notes, on "bestow", which is our choice, and the four parts are our own. This is both strains, each sung twice as the book marks it, for 2 to 4 singers. Courtesy Winterthur Museum, Garden & Library.',
    // Sources: song facts [Mary Hazzard's manuscript songbook, Winterthur Library, ASC 893, pages 16 to 20: no title, place, giver or date; the songs on either side signed "Second Family Canterbury", dated "Feb 22nd 1847" and "April 1847"]; song facts [the same songbook, page 18: "Who will turn and twist and real"; we sing "reel"]; story notes (seen): words as written, "Who will bow and bend like a willow, Who will turn and twist and reel"; three strains; songbook begun February 7th 1847; credits: words and tune Shaker song (Mary Hazzard's songbook, about 1847), arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: original, written for Stackapella; licence.source: the manuscript's pages 18 and 19, in Shaker letteral notation in C, sung in C with every value doubled, every pitch as written; three strains, each sung twice, as the repeat signs mark them; "Courtesy Winterthur Museum, Garden & Library."; description: the alto and tenor close beneath the tune, the bass holds the roots on "oo"; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'who-will-bow-and-bend' => 'Who Will Bow and Bend is a Shaker song in three strains: "Who will bow and bend like a willow, Who will turn and twist and reel". It is copied in Mary Hazzard\'s manuscript songbook, begun in 1847, with no title, place, giver or date; two songs away on each side are songs signed by Canterbury\'s Second Family and dated "Feb 22nd 1847" and "April 1847". Hazzard wrote "Who will turn and twist and real"; we sing "reel". Our tune and words are read from her page, written in the Shakers\' notation of letters, in C, with every pitch as written, and the four parts are our own. This is all three strains, each sung twice as the book marks it, for 2 to 4 singers. Courtesy Winterthur Museum, Garden & Library.',
    // Sources: song facts [Mary Hazzard's manuscript songbook, Winterthur Library, ASC 893, page 23: no title, only the line "Sung by Jack thro Abram P. on the holy Mount Aug 30th"]; song facts [the same page: its year from the song above it, "August 30th 1847. Sung by the Saviour on the Holy Mount thro Abm Perkins"]; story notes (seen): words "I am fill'd with heavenly treasures" (twice), "Praise praise praise praise"; songbook begun February 7th 1847; credits: words and tune Shaker song (Mary Hazzard's songbook, about 1847), arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: original, written for Stackapella; licence.source: the manuscript's page 23, in Shaker letteral notation in A minor in 6/8, sung a fourth lower, in E minor, every pitch as written; both strains sung twice, as the repeat signs mark them; the dashes after "treasures" and "praise" sung as the last syllable held through the wordless notes; "Courtesy Winterthur Museum, Garden & Library."; description: in a lilting 6/8; the soprano carries the last word through a run of wordless notes while the alto and tenor sing "oo" beneath; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'heavenly-treasures' => 'I Am Fill\'d with Heavenly Treasures is a short Shaker song of joy: "I am fill\'d with heavenly treasures", then "Praise, praise, praise, praise". It survives in Mary Hazzard\'s manuscript songbook, begun in 1847, with no title, only the line "Sung by Jack thro Abram P. on the holy Mount Aug 30th". Its year comes from the song above it on the same page: "August 30th 1847. Sung by the Savior on the Holy Mount thro Abm Perkins". Our tune and words are read from her page, written in the Shakers\' notation of letters, a fourth lower, in E minor, with every pitch as written, and the four parts are our own. This is both strains, each sung twice as the book marks it, for 2 to 4 singers, in a lilting 6/8. Courtesy Winterthur Museum, Garden & Library.',
    // Sources: song facts [Antonio García Cubas, El libro de mis recuerdos (Mexico City, 1904), p. 547, read from the scan: riding by moonlight up to the plain of Atotonilco el Grande; outside a house, as day came near, they began "cantar alegremente las mañanitas", "Despierta, mi bien despierta", and soon the windows opened]; song facts [Anita Brenner, Idols Behind Altars (Harcourt, Brace, 1929), p. 153, read from the scan: at the Guadalupe fiesta the Virgin is serenaded with the mañanitas, which belong to her and also to sweethearts and to young girls on their birthday mornings (our paraphrase: Brenner died in 1974)]; licence.words: traditional Mexican, printed unsigned in El laúd hispano-americano (Los Angeles: C. Santos, 1921); verse 2 and the refrain's first two lines already printed in 1904 by García Cubas; verse 1 first seen in print in this 1921 book, no earlier print found on an open scan; licence.tune: printed as a folk song by Albert Friedenthal in 1913 and, with its words, by García Cubas in 1904; credits: words and tune traditional Mexican, arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: original, written for Stackapella; licence.source: tune from Friedenthal, Musik, Tanz und Dichtung bei den Kreolen Amerikas (1913), pp. 61 to 62, "Die Mañanitas ... auch Amapola genannt", without words, in A major, written here in G; words from El laúd hispano-americano (1921), pp. 104 to 105, its first and second stanzas as verses 1 and 2 and its third as the refrain after each; the harmony our own; description: two verses, each followed by the refrain "Despierta, mi bien, despierta"; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'las-mananitas' => 'Las Mañanitas is the Mexican dawn serenade: "Despierta, mi bien, despierta", wake up, my dear, wake up. In his memoirs of 1904 Antonio García Cubas tells how, outside a house, las mañanitas were sung as day came near, and soon the windows opened; his book prints the tune with our second verse and the start of our refrain, their earliest known printing. Writing of the Guadalupe fiesta in 1929, Anita Brenner described how the Virgin is serenaded there with the mañanitas, which she said belong to the Mother and also to sweethearts and to young girls on the morning of their birthday. Our tune is read from Albert Friedenthal\'s printing of 1913, moved from his A major to G, and our words from El laúd hispano-americano (Los Angeles, 1921), the earliest printing of our first verse that we have found. The four parts are our own: two verses, each followed by the refrain, in Spanish, for 2 to 4 singers.',
    // Sources: song facts [Rafael Mitjana, Cincuenta y cuatro canciones españolas del siglo XVI (Uppsala, 1909), introduction, and Mitjana in Lavignac, Encyclopédie de la musique, part I, vol. 4 (Paris, 1920), p. 2006, read from the scans: he came across the 1556 book in Uppsala University's library and believed it the only known copy]; song facts [Villancicos de diversos autores (Venice: Girolamo Scotto, 1556), fols. 42v to 43r, read from the library's photograph on Wikimedia Commons: the bass sings the refrain alone first, then all four voices sing it, and the verses are the bass's alone, "solo las coblas"]; story notes (seen): Gombert the book's only named composer; gloss: "nuestra cordera", our ewe-lamb; "garçones", young lads, here angels; credits: words and tune anonymous, from Villancicos de diversos autores (Venice, 1556), the tune sometimes attributed to Mateo Flecha the Elder, arrangement the 1556 print's own four voices, adapted by Stackapella; licence.arrangement: a semitone above the print's pitch; the bass's opening solo and its solo verse sung by the soprano an octave up; the leading note raised at the cadences; licence.source: the call, the refrain and verses 1 ("El lobo rabioso") and 2 ("Yo vi mil garçones") read from the print; description: the soprano sings the opening call and two verses alone, everyone answers "Riu, riu, chiu, la guarda ribera" in harmony; light and quick, in E-flat minor; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'riu-riu-chiu' => 'Riu, Riu, Chiu is a Spanish Christmas villancico from Villancicos de diversos autores, a book printed in Venice in 1556 that names no composer for it. Rafael Mitjana came across the book in Uppsala University\'s library and believed it the only known copy; it is now called the Cancionero de Upsala, or the Cancionero del Duque de Calabria. The refrain sings that God kept the wolf from "nuestra cordera", our ewe-lamb, and verse 2 sees a thousand young lads, the angels, singing of glory in heaven and peace on earth. In the 1556 print the verses are the bass\'s alone: "solo las coblas". Our arrangement is the print\'s own four voices, a semitone higher, in E-flat minor, with the bass\'s solo call and verses sung by the soprano an octave up, the leading note raised at the cadences, the tenor\'s "Dios guar-" started on the bass\'s B-flat, and a few closing notes changed so the last chords keep their third. This is the call and two verses, each followed by the refrain everyone sings, in old Spanish, for 2 to 4 singers.',
    // Sources: song facts [El Himnario evangélico, para el uso de todas las iglesias (New York: Sociedad Americana de Tratados, copyright 1893), No. 48, p. 38, read from the scan: the earliest known printing of these Spanish words; the tune headed "Holy Night", with no one named, not Gruber, not Mohr, not the writer of the Spanish]; song facts [El Himnario evangélico (New York, 1893), No. 48; Himnos evangélicos (New York, 1894), No. 48; Himnario provisional (Philadelphia, 1907), No. 18; El Nuevo himnario evangélico (New York, 1915), No. 83, p. 32; read from the scans: verse 2's second line "En el campo al pastor" in 1893, 1894 and 1907; the earliest printing we have found of "Oye humilde el fiel pastor" in 1915]; credits: Spanish words after Joseph Mohr's German (1816), as printed, unsigned, in El Himnario evangélico (New York, 1893), usually credited to Federico Fliedner; tune Franz Xaver Gruber (1818); arrangement Stackapella; licence.words: the credit to Federico Fliedner a lead, not confirmed on any page seen; licence.arrangement: original, written for Stackapella; the 1893 hymnal's four parts unsigned and used only to check the chords; licence.source: the tune as printed, in C in 6/8; verse 1 as underlaid and verses 2 and 3 as printed below it; description: all three verses, gentle and lilting in 6/8; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'noche-de-paz' => 'Noche de paz is Silent Night in Spanish, after Joseph Mohr\'s German words of 1816, to Franz Xaver Gruber\'s tune of 1818. The earliest known printing of these Spanish words is in El Himnario evangélico (New York, 1893). It calls the tune by an English name, "Holy Night", and names no one: not Gruber, not Mohr, nor whoever wrote the Spanish. The words are often credited to Federico Fliedner, in 13 of the 18 hymnals from 1990 on that Hymnary.org indexes, but no printing we have seen before 1964 names him. In 1893 the second verse\'s second line was "En el campo al pastor"; today\'s "Oye humilde el fiel pastor" is first found, in what we have seen, in a New York hymnal of 1915. We sing all three verses of the 1893 hymnal, in C, lilting in 6/8, with four parts of our own, for 2 to 4 singers.',
    // Sources: song facts [Cánticos espirituales con música, recogidos por la "Revista Católica" (Las Vegas, New Mexico, 1916), the note "¡¡¡IMPORTANTE!!!" (leaf 9), read from the scan: it at last brings out the music of the "Cánticos espirituales, recogidos por el P. Ralliere"; words and music don't always keep the rules of the art, but the people of its region "así los conoce, así los canta, así los quiere", so it left them "intactos" as far as it could]; song facts [Florence Hawley Ellis, "Tomé and Father J. B. R.", New Mexico Historical Review 30, no. 2 (April 1955), p. 103, and no. 3 (July 1955), pp. 217 and 218, read from the scans: Ralliere, who collected the Cánticos espirituales, was brought from France and served Tomé, New Mexico, from 1858 to 1911]; story notes (seen): No. 23, "Pastores á Belén", pp. 20 and 21, under "Para la Noche Buena"; review corrections: the song unsigned, not "traditional"; "á" the older spelling of "a", sung the same; gloss: "a ver á nuestro bien", to see our good; "turrones", nougat; "Niño Manuel", the Child Emmanuel; credits: words and tune unsigned, as printed in Cánticos espirituales con música (Las Vegas, New Mexico, 1916), arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: original, written for Stackapella; the book's unsigned keyboard part used only to check the chords; licence.source: in F major in 6/8, written here a whole tone higher, in G; words as printed, accents included ("Pastores á Belén", "A ver á nuestro bien"); the one verse printed with the music, and the refrain; the book sends singers to its words-only editions (1908 and 1913) for further verses; description: the verse, "Allí, allí, allí, nos espera Jesús", and the refrain, "Llevemos, pues, turrones y miel"; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'pastores-a-belen' => 'Pastores a Belén calls the shepherds to Bethlehem "A ver á nuestro bien", to see our good, and its refrain brings "turrones y miel", nougat and honey, to the Child Manuel. The earliest printing of its music that we have seen, unsigned, is in Cánticos espirituales con música (Las Vegas, New Mexico, 1916). The book\'s note says it at last brings out the music of the Cánticos espirituales collected by Father J. B. Ralliere, who served Tomé, New Mexico, from 1858 to 1911, and that it leaves the songs "intactos" as far as it can, because the people of its region "así los conoce, así los canta, así los quiere" (knows them, sings them, loves them this way). We sing its page a tone higher, in G, keeping its "á" for "a", with four parts of our own: the verse and the refrain, for 2 to 4 singers.',
    // Sources: song facts [Lluís Millet, Pel nostre ideal (Barcelona, 1917), p. 209, read from the scan: introducing it in a lecture, he said it "té una de les melodíes més hermoses de la terra catalana"; his harmony for men's voices written "ja fa uns quants anys"]; song facts [Obra del Cançoner Popular de Catalunya, Materials, vol. I, fascicle II (Barcelona, 1928; the volume's title page says 1926), p. 380, read from the scan: "L'un des plus beaux noëls de notre chansonnier"; "Les contrebandiers" "véritablement transfigurée"]; story notes (seen): Cançoner popular (Barcelona, 1903), Cançó I, the same tune and form; gloss: "despuntar", to dawn; "lo major lluminar", the greater light, the sun; "ditxosa", blessed; "àliga imperial", imperial eagle; credits: words and tune traditional Catalan, as printed by Lluís Millet (1917); arrangement Lluís Millet (1867-1941), harmony printed 1917, adapted by Stackapella; licence.arrangement: Millet's harmony for solo voice and humming men's choir, written a tone below the print; his four choir parts become three hummed parts, each humming "oo" as his choir hums with closed mouth; licence.source: Pel nostre ideal: recull d'escrits de Lluís Millet, director de l'Orfeó Català, Exemple XIV, "El cant dels aucells", "Harm. de'n Lluís Millet", pp. 237 to 241, for solo and men's choir humming ("Boca tancada"), in G minor, sung here in F minor; verse 1 "Al veure despuntar", verse 2 the text's second stanza, "L'Aliga imperial"; old spelling and accents kept; the last three lines of each stanza sung twice, as in the print; checked against Cançoner popular (Barcelona, 1903), Cançó I, the same tune and form in another key; description: the soprano sings the tune while the alto, tenor and bass hum, two verses, unhurried, in F minor; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'el-cant-dels-ocells' => 'El cant dels ocells, the song of the birds, is a Catalan Christmas carol in which the birds go singing to celebrate the newborn Jesus; in verse 2 the imperial eagle sings "Jesús es nat per tráurens de pecat", Jesus is born to take us out of sin. The earliest printing of its tune that we have seen is the first song of Cançoner popular (Barcelona, 1903). Lluís Millet, director of the Orfeó Català, printed it in 1917 with his harmony for a solo voice over a men\'s choir humming with closed mouths, and said it has "una de les melodíes més hermoses de la terra catalana" (one of the most beautiful melodies of the Catalan land). Ours is Millet\'s harmony, his four humming parts made three, a tone lower, in F minor: a hummed opening and two verses, in Catalan, for 2 to 4 singers.',
    // Sources: story the-first-noel.md (Gilbert, Some Ancient Christmas Carols, 2nd ed., 1823, Carol IX: earliest known printing of the words; Sandys, Christmas Carols, Ancient and Modern, 1833: earliest known printing of the tune; Stainer's version fixed its familiar form; first series of Christmas Carols New and Old, 1867, Musical Times 1 December 1867); licence.source: Bramley and Stainer, Christmas Carols New and Old, first series (Novello, Ewer and Co.; this copy a later printing of the 1870s, catalogued [187?]), No. VI, "The First Nowell", pp. 12 and 13, four parts in D, 3/4, read from the scan at archive.org/details/christmascarolsn00staiiala (n17 and n18); credits: words and tune traditional English, arrangement John Stainer's four parts, adapted by Stackapella (Stainer died 1901); licence.arrangement: Stainer's tune and chords, the lower parts revoiced for beginners; sections: Verse 1, Chorus, Verse 2, Chorus; voicings: 2 soprano and alto; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'the-first-noel' => 'The First Noel is a traditional English carol of the shepherds, the star and the wise men. The earliest known printing of its words is in the second edition of Davies Gilbert\'s Some Ancient Christmas Carols, in 1823, and of its tune in William Sandys\'s Christmas Carols, Ancient and Modern, in 1833. John Stainer set it in four parts for Christmas Carols New and Old, the carol book he edited with H. R. Bramley, whose first series came out in 1867, and his version fixed the tune\'s familiar form. Our arrangement is read from his four parts in a printing of the 1870s, in D as printed, with the lower parts revoiced for beginners. This is verses 1 and 2 for 2 to 4 singers, the soprano on the tune, each verse followed by the chorus, "Nowell, Nowell", spelled as the book spells it.',
    // Sources: licence.source: Charles L. Hutchins, Carols Old and Carols New (Boston: The Parish Choir, 1916), Carol 599, p. 484, "L. H. Redner.", in G, read from the scan at archive.org/details/carolsoldcarolsn00huth (n515), checked against The Hymnal (Church Pension Fund, 1916), Hymn 78 (archive.org/details/authoriz00epis, n172); the earliest known printing with Redner's tune: The Church Porch, ed. William R. Huntington (New York: E. P. Dutton, 1874), No. 43, "St. Louis", in G (archive.org/details/churchporchservi00hunt, n112), with B on "and" in "deep and dreamless" where the 1916 books have D; credits: words Phillips Brooks (died 1893), tune Lewis H. Redner, "St. Louis" (died 1908), arrangement Redner's four parts adapted by Stackapella; licence.arrangement: the changes for beginners; sections: Verse 1, Verse 3, Verse 4; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'o-little-town-of-bethlehem' => 'O Little Town of Bethlehem is Phillips Brooks\'s carol to the tune St. Louis by Lewis H. Redner. Its earliest known printing with Redner\'s tune is in The Church Porch, a Sunday-school service book and hymnal published in New York in 1874, where one note of the tune differs from the later form we sing: B, not D, on "and" in "deep and dreamless". Our arrangement is read from Redner\'s four parts in Charles L. Hutchins\'s Carols Old and Carols New, printed in Boston in 1916, in G as printed: the soprano sings his tune, and the lower parts are adapted for beginners. This is verses 1, 3 and 4 of the four the book prints, for 2 to 4 singers: the sleeping town, the silent gift, and the prayer to the holy Child.',
    // Sources: credits: English words by James Chadwick (died 1882); story [The Holy Family Hymns (London, 1860), No. 9, "Christmas Hymn", credited to Rev. J. Chadwick; The Crown of Jesus (London, 1862), the opening sung today];
    'angels-we-have-heard-on-high' => 'Angels We Have Heard on High is the English form of a French carol, Les anges dans nos campagnes, sung with its refrain left in Latin: Gloria in excelsis Deo, glory to God in the highest. Its English words began with James Chadwick, in a London hymn book of 1860, and The Crown of Jesus, a Catholic prayer book printed in London in 1862, gave them the opening sung today. Our tune and words are read from Charles L. Hutchins\'s Carols Old and Carols New, printed in Boston in 1916, where it is headed Old French Carol, and written here a tone higher, in G: the soprano sings his tune, and the alto, tenor and bass are our own, checked against the chords of the four-part setting on his page. This is verses 1 to 3 of his four, for 2 to 4 singers, each followed by the Gloria, where the tune\'s long run on "Glo-" falls over the others\' held notes.',
    // Sources: story good-king-wenceslas.md (draft, under check: earliest known printing the folio Carols for Christmas-tide, 1853, out by the 1854 book's Notice of SS. Simon and Jude, 1853; Piae Cantiones, Greifswald, 1582); licence.source: The Condensed Vocal Parts to the Carols for Christmas-tide, Helmore and Neale (London: J. Alfred Novello, 1854), No. XI, "Good King Wenceslas", pp. 37 to 40, in four parts in A, every verse under the music, read from the scan on Wikimedia Commons (from HathiTrust record 011630024); its Notice, dated SS. Simon and Jude (28 October) 1853, refers to the folio edition already out; the chords checked against Bramley and Stainer, Christmas Carols New and Old, first series, No. X, pp. 20 and 21 (archive.org/details/christmascarolsn00staiiala, n25 and n26), unsigned; credits: words John Mason Neale (died 1866), tune the spring carol "Tempus adest floridum" (Piae Cantiones, 1582) as Thomas Helmore (died 1890) set it, arrangement Stackapella; licence.arrangement: our own alto, tenor and bass under the tune as Neale and Helmore print it; sections: Verse 1, Verse 4, Verse 5; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'good-king-wenceslas' => 'Good King Wenceslas is John Mason Neale\'s carol of the king who goes out with his page on the Feast of Stephen to carry meat, wine and pine logs to a poor man, sung to the spring carol Tempus adest floridum from the songbook Piae Cantiones of 1582. Its earliest known printing is Neale and Thomas Helmore\'s Carols for Christmas-tide, out by 28 October 1853, and our tune and words are read from their 1854 vocal parts, in A as printed: the soprano sings their tune, and the alto, tenor and bass are our own, checked against the chords of Bramley and Stainer\'s Christmas Carols New and Old. This is verses 1, 4 and 5 of the five, for 2 to 4 singers: the poor man seen in the moonlit snow, the page\'s fear and the king\'s answer, and the warm footprints with the closing call to bless the poor.',
    // Sources: licence.source: Cecil J. Sharp, English Folk-Carols (London: Novello and Simpkin; Taunton: Barnicott and Pearce, the Wessex Press, 1911), No. VII, pp. 17 and 18, voice and piano in G, 3/4, read from the scan at archive.org/details/englishfolkcarol00shar (n34 and n35); Sharp's note, pp. 63 and 64 (n80 and n81): "Sung by Mrs. Mary Clayton, at Chipping Campden", Mrs Clayton's words supplemented with another set recited to Sharp by the late Mrs Wyatt of East Harptree, Somerset, stanza 2's line "To be our sweet Saviour" the broadside reading; credits: words and tune traditional English, as Sharp printed them (Sharp died 1924), arrangement Stackapella (Sharp's piano part read, not used); sections: Verse 1, Refrain, Verse 2, Refrain, Verse 3, Refrain; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'the-holly-and-the-ivy' => 'The Holly and the Ivy is an English folk carol that Cecil Sharp printed in his English Folk-Carols of 1911 as he noted it from Mrs Mary Clayton at Chipping Campden, filling out her words with another set recited to him by the late Mrs Wyatt of East Harptree, Somerset. Our tune and words are read from that book, in G as printed: the soprano sings Sharp\'s tune, and the alto, tenor and bass are our own, with Sharp\'s piano part read but not used. This is verses 1 to 3 of the six, for 2 to 4 singers, the holly\'s crown, its white blossom and its red berry, each with the refrain "The rising of the sun".',
    // Sources: licence.source: John H. Hopkins, Jr., Carols, Hymns, and Songs (New York: Church Book Depository, 762 Broadway; the first edition, its title page undated, catalogued [1863?], the earliest printing we have seen), "Three Kings of Orient", pp. 12 and 13, three singers' staves headed GASPARD, MELCHIOR and BALTHAZAR, in E minor turning to G major, 3/8, read from the scan at archive.org/details/carolshym00hopk (n19 and n20), the same music in the 1872 and 1882 printings; Hopkins's note giving verses 1 and 5 to all three kings; credits: words and tune John Henry Hopkins Jr. (died 1891), arrangement his own parts adapted by Stackapella; sections: Verse 1, Chorus, Verse 5, Chorus; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'we-three-kings' => 'We Three Kings of Orient Are is John Henry Hopkins Jr.\'s carol, words and tune his own. The earliest printing we have seen is in his book Carols, Hymns, and Songs, published in New York without a date and catalogued, with a question mark, as 1863, where it is titled Three Kings of Orient and has three singers\' parts headed Gaspard, Melchior and Balthazar. Our arrangement is read from that printing, in E minor turning to G major as printed, and keeps its "Hallelujah", where his editions of 1872 and 1882 print "Alleluia": the soprano sings his tune, and the alto, tenor and bass are adapted from his trio, its accompaniment and his four-part Chorus, with a tenor of our own in the verse, for 2 to 4 beginners. This is verses 1 and 5, the two his book gives to all three kings together, each followed by the Chorus, "O Star of Wonder".',
    // Sources: licence.source: Charles L. Hutchins, Carols Old and Carols New (Boston: The Parish Choir, 1916), Carol 607, p. 491, "Words by Rev. E. H. Sears", "R. S. Willis", in four parts in B-flat, 6/8, read from the scan at archive.org/details/carolsoldcarolsn00huth (n522), verse 3 printed below the music with Hutchins's "O ye" where Sears has "And ye"; the earliest known printing of the words with this tune: Hymns and Songs of Praise, ed. Roswell D. Hitchcock, Zachary Eddy and Philip Schaff (New York, 1874), No. 158, "Christmas Carol", tune "CAROL. C. M.", p. 66, credited "Richard Storrs Willis", in B-flat, 6/8 (archive.org/details/hymnssongso00hitc, n73); the words' earliest known printing: the Christian Register (Boston), 29 December 1849 (the story); credits: words Edmund Hamilton Sears (died 1876), tune Richard Storrs Willis, "Carol" (died 1900; 1850, full form by 1869, the story), arrangement Willis's four parts adapted by Stackapella; licence.arrangement: the changes for beginners; sections: Verse 1, Verse 2, Verse 3; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'it-came-upon-the-midnight-clear' => 'It Came Upon the Midnight Clear is Edmund Hamilton Sears\'s carol to the tune Carol by Richard Storrs Willis. The earliest known printing of its words is in the Christian Register, a Boston paper, on 29 December 1849, and of the words with Willis\'s tune in Hymns and Songs of Praise, published in New York in 1874. Our arrangement is read from Willis\'s four parts in Charles L. Hutchins\'s Carols Old and Carols New, printed in Boston in 1916, in B-flat and six-eight as printed: the soprano sings his tune, and the lower parts are adapted for beginners. This is verses 1 to 3 of the four Hutchins prints, Sears\'s stanzas 1, 2 and 4, for 2 to 4 singers: the angels\' song of peace on a midnight long ago, their song still floating over the weary world, and the call to all who toil to rest and hear the angels sing, with Hutchins\'s "O ye" where Sears has "And ye".',
    // Sources: story in-the-bleak-midwinter.md (checked: the words' earliest known printing Scribner's Monthly, New York, January 1872, p. 278; Holst composed and harmonised Cranham for The English Hymnal, 1906; Vaughan Williams the book's music editor); licence.source: The English Hymnal with Tunes (London: Henry Frowde, Oxford University Press, and A. R. Mowbray; undated, the Frowde imprint of 1906 to 1913, the preface dated Ascension Day 1906, its music preface signed "R. Vaughan Williams, Musical Editor"), No. 25, "Cranham. (Irreg.)", "G. von Holst", pp. 44 and 45, in four parts in F, 4/4, the five verses headed "Christina G. Rossetti, 1830-94", read from the scan at archive.org/details/englishhymnalwit0000unse_b6d2 (n75 and n76), with verse 5's misprint "Pcor" sung as "Poor"; credits: words Christina G. Rossetti (died 1894), tune Gustav Holst, "Cranham" (died 1934), arrangement Holst's four parts adapted by Stackapella; firstPublished: 1906, GB; licence.arrangement: the changes for beginners; sections: Verse 1, Verse 2, Verse 5; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'in-the-bleak-midwinter' => 'In the Bleak Midwinter is Christina Rossetti\'s Christmas poem, whose earliest known printing is in Scribner\'s Monthly, a New York magazine, in January 1872, sung to Cranham, the tune Gustav Holst composed and harmonised for The English Hymnal, the hymn book whose music Ralph Vaughan Williams edited, its preface dated 1906. Our arrangement is read from that book, in F as printed: the soprano sings Holst\'s tune, and his alto, tenor and bass are adapted for beginners. This is the first, second and last of the five verses the book prints, for 2 to 4 singers: the frozen winter, the God whom heaven cannot hold, for whom a stable-place sufficed, and the question "What can I give him" with its answer, "Give my heart", singing "Poor" where the book misprints "Pcor".',
    // Sources: licence.source: Thomas Sharp, A Dissertation on the Pageants or Dramatic Mysteries Anciently Performed at Coventry (Coventry: Merridew and Son, 1825), the words of "Song II" on pp. 113 and 114 under "Theise songes belonge to the Taylors & Shearemens Pagant ... the second or middlemost the women singe", the music on the engraved plates numbered 116 and 117 ("The treble", "The tenor", "The basse", in the old notation), the leaf dated 13 May 1591, read from archive.org/details/adissertationonthepageants (n142 to n148), checked against archive.org/details/dissertationonpa00sharuoft (n264, n266); the manuscripts' loss in the 1879 Birmingham fire: Hardin Craig, Two Coventry Corpus Christi Plays (1902), p. xi, read as OCR text (a lead for the story's check); credits: the women's song from the Pageant of the Shearmen and Tailors, the anonymous setting of 1591, as Sharp printed them (Sharp died 1841), arrangement the 1591 parts adapted by Stackapella with a tenor of our own; licence.arrangement: the changes for beginners and the tenor; sections: Refrain, Verse 1, Verse 2, Verse 3, Refrain; voicings: 2 soprano and tenor, 3 soprano, tenor and bass; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'coventry-carol' => 'The Coventry Carol is the women\'s song from the Pageant of the Shearmen and Tailors, one of the plays once performed at Coventry, a lullaby for the young children Herod has ordered killed. Its words and its three-part setting, on a leaf dated 13 May 1591, survive because Thomas Sharp printed them in 1825, the music as an engraving of the old leaf; the manuscripts were later lost. Our arrangement is read from Sharp\'s plates, in G minor as printed: the soprano sings the 1591 treble, the tune, the alto the 1591 tenor and the bass the 1591 bass, all adapted for beginners, and the tenor is our own, so each chord keeps its third with 2 or 3 singers too. This is the refrain, in the short form sung today, the three verses and the refrain again, for 2 to 4 singers, with the words in their old spellings.',
    // Sources: licence.source: Bramley and Stainer, Christmas Carols New and Old, first series (London: Novello, Ewer and Co.; catalogued [187?], its title page advertising the second series, so 1871 or later), No. XIV, "What Child is this?", pp. 32 and 33, four parts in E minor, 6/8, read from archive.org/details/christmascarolsn00staiiala (n37, n38); compared with a later printing of the complete book, archive.org/details/christmascarolsn00lond (n37, n38; its index: words "W. C. Dix", air "Old English"); the first series out in November 1867: Musical Times, 1 December 1867, Novello's "During the last month" and the advert on p. 235, "14. What Child is this? ... Old English" (the story's research); the four parts unsigned (other carols in the index are marked "Har. by J. S."); credits: words William Chatterton Dix (died 9 September 1898: Musical Times, 1 October 1898, p. 659; Julian), tune Greensleeves, Old English, arrangement Stackapella on the book's harmony (editors Stainer, died 1901, and Bramley, died 1917); written a whole tone up, F-sharp minor; licence.arrangement: the parts and the breaths; sections: Verse 1, Chorus, Verse 3, Chorus; voicings: 2 soprano and alto, 3 soprano, alto and bass; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'what-child-is-this' => 'What Child Is This? is William Chatterton Dix\'s Christmas carol to the old English tune Greensleeves, as Henry Ramsden Bramley and John Stainer printed it in the first series of their Christmas Carols New and Old, published by Novello in London in November 1867; we read it in a later printing. Our arrangement is read from that book, a whole tone higher in F-sharp minor: the soprano sings the book\'s tune, with breaths written in for beginners, and the alto, tenor and bass are our own, on the chords of the book\'s four unsigned parts. This is verses 1 and 3 for 2 to 4 singers, each with its own chorus: the Child asleep on Mary\'s lap, with "This, this is Christ the King", and the gifts of incense, gold and myrrh, with "Raise, raise, the song on high".',
    // Sources: licence.source: Hymns Ancient and Modern, Revised Edition, No. 49, pp. 34 and 35 (the Revised Edition of 1875, its preface dated 21 January 1875, in a later printing: the printer "William Clowes and Sons, Limited", 1880 or later; the copy catalogued 1874, New York: Pott, Young), the tune and words, barred in four minims, in E minor, read from archive.org/details/hymnsancientmode00monk (n49, n50), checked against the 1890 London printing (hymnsancientmode0000unse_s2j4, n50); the harmony: W. H. Monk's four parts in the 1861 first edition, No. 36, "Harmonized or Arranged by W. H. Monk" in its Index of Tunes (archive.org/details/hymnsancientmode00unse, n65, n23); the Hymnal Noted, No. 65, "Draw nigh, draw nigh, Emmanuel" (scan seen); the Latin hymn and Neale's 1851 translation: Julian, Dictionary of Hymnology (1907 printing), "O Antiphonae" and "Neale, John Mason", p. 786; credits: words Neale (died 1866), tune Veni Emmanuel from the Hymnal Noted (Helmore died 1890), arrangement Monk's four parts adapted by Stackapella (Monk died 1889); licence.arrangement: the changes for beginners; sections: Verse 1, Refrain, Verse 2, Refrain, Verse 3, Refrain; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'o-come-o-come-emmanuel' => 'O Come, O Come, Emmanuel is the Advent hymn that John Mason Neale translated from the Latin "Veni, veni, Emmanuel", sung to the tune Veni Emmanuel from the Hymnal Noted, whose musical editor was Thomas Helmore. There Neale\'s words begin "Draw nigh, draw nigh, Emmanuel". The earliest printing we have read that opens "O come, O come, Emmanuel" is Hymns Ancient and Modern of 1861. Our tune and words are read from that book\'s revised edition of 1875, in a later printing, in E minor as printed, and our harmony from W. H. Monk\'s four parts in the 1861 edition: the soprano sings the tune, and the lower parts are adapted for beginners. This is verses 1 to 3 of the five the book prints, for 2 to 4 singers, each with the refrain "Rejoice! Rejoice! Emmanuel": captive Israel waiting, the Rod of Jesse, and the Dayspring that puts death\'s dark shadows to flight.',
    // Sources: licence.source: H. R. Bramley and John Stainer, Christmas Carols New and Old (London: Novello and Company, Limited; the complete book of 70 carols, undated, its title page naming "Sir John Stainer"; catalogued [1880]), No. 64, "I saw three Ships", Briskly, pp. 164 and 165, in four parts in G, 6/8, read from archive.org/details/christmascarolsn00lond (n169, n170); its index, p. 182 (n187): words "Traditional", air "Traditional (Derbyshire). Harm. by J. S."; verses 1, 2, 3 and 9 of the nine printed; the earliest printing we read of these words with this tune: William Henry Husk, Songs of the Nativity (London: Hotten; undated, catalogued 1884, but 1868 by the DNB, 1891, and the Oxford Book of Carols, 1928), p. 190, "On Christmas Day in the Morning", in A, verse 1 (firstPublished 1868); Sandys, Christmas Carols, Ancient and Modern (1833), pp. 112 and 113 and air No. 9, an earlier 3/4 form; No. 64 first in the third series, out 15 October 1878 (Musical Times, 1 October 1878, p. 565) (the story's research); credits: words and tune traditional English, the tune from Derbyshire, arrangement Stackapella after Stainer's harmony (Stainer died 1901; Bramley 1917); licence.arrangement: the changes for beginners and the breaths; sections: Verse 1, Verse 2, Verse 3, Verse 9; song file (the parts' guide notes)
    'i-saw-three-ships' => 'I Saw Three Ships is a traditional English carol, sung here to its tune from Derbyshire as Henry Ramsden Bramley and John Stainer printed it, with Stainer\'s four parts, in the complete edition of their Christmas Carols New and Old, published by Novello in London. William Sandys printed its words in 1833 with an earlier form of the tune in three-four time; the earliest printing we have read of the six-eight tune we sing is William Henry Husk\'s Songs of the Nativity of 1868, under the title On Christmas Day in the Morning, and Stainer\'s setting first appeared in the third series of Christmas Carols New and Old in 1878. Our arrangement is read from Bramley and Stainer\'s book, in G as printed: the soprano sings the tune, with breaths written in for beginners, and the alto, tenor and bass are adapted from Stainer\'s harmony. This is four of the book\'s nine verses, for 2 to 4 singers: the three ships, what was in them, the Virgin Mary and Christ aboard, and the last, "Then let us all rejoice amain".',
];
